/* Lessons for ISACA CISA (2024 job practice): one per plan topic, matched by exact topic text.
   How to write and check them: docs/LESSON_GUIDE.md */
CertHub.addLessons("cisa", [
  { t: "IS audit standards, guidelines and the ISACA Code of Professional Ethics",
    body: [
      "An IS auditor gives other people confidence that information systems are controlled. That confidence is only worth something if the audit is done to a recognized standard by someone who is honest and independent. ISACA publishes the IT Audit Framework (ITAF), which contains mandatory standards, guidelines that explain how to apply them, and tools and techniques. The CISA exam expects you to think like someone who follows these standards by default.",
      "The standards fall into three groups. General standards cover the audit charter, organizational independence, auditor objectivity, reasonable expectation that the audit can be completed, due professional care, proficiency and assertions. Performance standards cover risk assessment and planning, supervision, materiality, evidence, using the work of other experts, and irregularities. Reporting standards cover the report itself and follow-up activities. Guidelines are not mandatory, but an auditor who departs from them should be able to justify why.",
      "Independence has two parts. Organizational independence means the audit function reports to a level, usually the audit committee of the board, that lets it work without interference. Independence in fact and appearance means the individual auditor has no conflicting interest or role. Typical threats are self-review (auditing your own work), management participation (making decisions that belong to management) and familiarity. When independence is impaired, the auditor discloses it to the appropriate parties rather than quietly carrying on.",
      "The ISACA Code of Professional Ethics asks members to support standards and controls, perform duties objectively and with due care, serve stakeholders lawfully, keep information confidential unless disclosure is legally required, stay competent, inform the appropriate parties of the results of work, and support education of the public. Breaking the code can lead to disciplinary action, including losing the certification. On the exam, the ethical answer is usually the one that reports honestly through the proper channel."
    ],
    terms: [["ITAF", "ISACA's IT Audit Framework: mandatory standards, supporting guidelines, and tools and techniques for IS audit and assurance work."], ["Independence in appearance", "The absence of circumstances that would lead a reasonable third party to doubt the auditor's objectivity."], ["Due professional care", "Applying the skill and diligence that a prudent, competent auditor would use in the same circumstances."], ["Self-review threat", "The risk that an auditor will not critically evaluate work they performed or designed themselves."]],
    example: "A newly hired auditor previously configured the company's identity management system as a consultant. When assigned to audit it, she tells the chief audit executive about her earlier role, and the audit is reassigned to a colleague while she audits a different area.",
    tip: "When a question involves an independence problem, the answer is almost always to disclose it to audit management or the audit committee, not to carry on or to narrow the scope quietly.",
    check: [["Are ITAF guidelines mandatory?", "No. The standards are mandatory; guidelines explain how to apply them, and departures from guidelines should be justified."], ["What should an auditor do when independence is impaired?", "Disclose the impairment to the appropriate parties, such as audit management, and let them decide whether to reassign the work."]] },
  { t: "Types of audits, assessments and reviews: IS, compliance, financial, operational, integrated, forensic",
    body: [
      "Not every audit asks the same question. Before you plan any work, you need to know what kind of engagement it is, because that decides the objective, the criteria and the evidence you need. The CISA exam often describes an engagement and expects you to recognize its type.",
      "A financial audit gives an opinion on whether financial statements are fairly presented; IS auditors support it by testing the IT controls behind financial systems. A compliance audit tests whether the organization follows specific laws, regulations, contracts or policies, for example a payment card standard. An operational audit evaluates whether a process is efficient and effective. An IS audit evaluates the controls over information systems: general IT controls such as access and change management, and application controls inside a system. An integrated audit combines financial, operational and IS work on one process so the auditor sees the whole control picture.",
      "A forensic audit or investigation is different in purpose: it gathers evidence about suspected fraud or crime in a way that could stand up in court, so evidence handling and chain of custody matter far more. Specialized reviews include administrative audits (efficiency of administrative processes) and third-party or service organization reviews, such as reading a SOC report on a cloud provider.",
      "Assessments and reviews are not always audits. A control self-assessment (CSA) has the process owners evaluate their own controls in facilitated workshops; it builds ownership and awareness but does not replace independent audit. A risk assessment identifies and rates risks rather than testing controls. A review or agreed-upon procedures engagement gives lower, more limited assurance than a full audit. Knowing the level of assurance each gives helps you decide how much you can rely on it."
    ],
    terms: [["Integrated audit", "An audit that combines financial, operational and IS audit work to evaluate all the controls over a process or system together."], ["Control self-assessment (CSA)", "A technique in which process owners and staff evaluate their own controls, usually in facilitated workshops."], ["Forensic audit", "An engagement to collect and analyze evidence about suspected fraud or crime for possible legal or disciplinary use."], ["Compliance audit", "An audit that tests adherence to specific laws, regulations, contracts or internal policies."]],
    example: "A retailer asks internal audit to review its online payment process. The team runs an integrated audit: financial auditors test revenue recognition, while IS auditors test access to the payment application, change management and the interface to the general ledger, and the report gives one combined view.",
    tip: "Control self-assessment does not replace audit. If an answer suggests CSA removes the need for independent testing, it is wrong; CSA supplements audit and improves ownership.",
    check: [["Which audit type needs the strictest evidence handling, and why?", "A forensic audit, because its evidence may be used in legal proceedings and must be shown to be unaltered."], ["What is the main benefit of control self-assessment?", "It makes process owners responsible for their controls and raises awareness, which can help detect risks early."]] },
  { t: "Risk-based audit planning: audit universe, risk assessment, annual plan and engagement scope",
    body: [
      "No audit function can review everything every year. Risk-based audit planning is how you decide where limited audit hours will do the most good. ISACA's standards require the auditor to use a risk-based approach, and the exam rewards answers that start with understanding the business and its risks.",
      "Planning happens at two levels. At the annual level, the audit function lists its audit universe, meaning every auditable area such as business processes, applications, infrastructure and third parties. It then rates each area for risk, considering factors like financial impact, regulatory exposure, complexity, recent changes, past findings and management's own risk assessments. The highest-risk areas get audited more often and more deeply. The resulting annual plan is approved by the audit committee and revised when risks change.",
      "At the engagement level, the auditor first gains an understanding of the business process, its objectives, systems and key risks. From that, the auditor sets the audit objectives and scope, identifies key controls, and decides the nature, timing and extent of testing. This is where the audit risk model helps. Inherent risk is the risk before any controls. Control risk is the chance controls will not prevent or detect an error. Detection risk is the chance the auditor's own procedures miss it. The auditor can only change detection risk, by doing more or better testing when inherent and control risks are high.",
      "Materiality also shapes planning. In IS audit, materiality is not only a dollar threshold; a weakness can be material because of what it could allow, such as a flaw that exposes sensitive data or lets someone bypass approvals across all transactions."
    ],
    terms: [["Audit universe", "The complete list of auditable areas in the organization, used as the starting point for risk-based planning."], ["Inherent risk", "The risk of an error or loss before considering any controls."], ["Detection risk", "The risk that the auditor's procedures fail to detect a material error; the only part of audit risk the auditor controls."], ["Materiality", "The significance of a weakness or error, judged by its potential effect on decisions or objectives, not only its dollar value."]],
    example: "A bank's audit team scores 60 auditable areas. The new mobile banking platform scores high for customer data exposure, recent launch and regulatory attention, so it is scheduled for this year with a deep scope; a stable internal wiki scores low and is placed on a three-year cycle.",
    tip: "If asked what the auditor should do first when planning, choose understanding the business and assessing risk. Choosing tests or tools comes later.",
    check: [["Which component of audit risk can the auditor reduce directly?", "Detection risk, by changing the nature, timing and extent of audit procedures."], ["Why can a small-dollar IT weakness still be material?", "Because of what it could enable, such as unauthorized access to all customer records or bypassing controls on every transaction."]] },
  { t: "Types of controls: preventive, detective, corrective, compensating; general vs application controls",
    body: [
      "A control is anything management puts in place to give reasonable assurance that objectives are met and risks are kept acceptable. Auditors classify controls so they can judge whether the mix is sensible and decide how to test each one.",
      "By function, preventive controls stop a problem before it happens: segregation of duties, input validation, access controls, approvals. Detective controls find problems after they occur: reconciliations, log reviews, exception reports, hash totals. Corrective controls fix the problem and reduce its impact: backup restoration, incident response, contingency plans. Deterrent controls discourage bad behavior, such as warning banners, and directive controls tell people what to do, such as policies. A compensating control is an alternative that reduces risk when the ideal control is not practical, for example supervisory review of logs when a small team cannot fully segregate duties.",
      "By scope, IT general controls (ITGCs) apply across many systems: logical access, change management, IT operations, backup and recovery, and system development. Application controls are built into a specific application and cover input, processing and output, such as edit checks, calculation checks and output reconciliation. Application controls can only be relied on if the general controls around them work. If anyone can change the program code without approval, a perfect edit check today may be gone tomorrow.",
      "Controls can also be manual or automated. Automated controls are consistent and, once tested and protected by good change management, can often be tested with a small sample or a single test of one instance. Manual controls depend on people, vary more, and need larger samples."
    ],
    terms: [["Compensating control", "An alternative control that reduces risk to an acceptable level when the primary control cannot be implemented."], ["IT general control (ITGC)", "A control over the IT environment, such as access or change management, that supports all applications running in it."], ["Application control", "A control inside a specific application that ensures complete, accurate and authorized input, processing and output."], ["Detective control", "A control that identifies errors or irregularities after they have occurred."]],
    example: "A payroll system has an automated check that rejects salaries above a limit. The auditor first tests change management and access to the program library; only after confirming nobody can alter the check without approval does she rely on one test of the automated control.",
    tip: "Weak general controls undermine application controls. If a question asks what to test first, IT general controls such as change management usually come before relying on automated application controls.",
    check: [["Classify a daily reconciliation of bank transactions to the ledger.", "Detective, because it identifies differences after processing has occurred."], ["Why can an automated control often be tested with a very small sample?", "It performs the same way every time, provided general controls such as change management keep it from being altered."]] },
  { t: "Audit project management: objectives, audit program, fieldwork, resourcing and independence",
    body: [
      "Every audit engagement is a small project with a scope, a timeline, staff and deliverables. The current CISA outline expects you to apply project management discipline to audits, so the work finishes on time, stays in scope and produces reliable conclusions.",
      "An engagement usually moves through these phases: planning, where you understand the area, assess risk and set objectives and scope; developing the audit program, which lists the procedures to test each key control; fieldwork, where you perform tests and collect evidence in workpapers; and reporting and follow-up. An engagement letter or memo confirms scope, timing, access and contacts with the auditee. Workpapers record what was done, by whom, what evidence was obtained and what was concluded, so a reviewer can re-trace the reasoning.",
      "Resourcing means making sure the team has the skills the scope requires. If the audit covers a cloud container platform and nobody on the team understands it, the auditor must train, bring in a guest auditor or use an outside expert. When using another expert's work, the auditor must evaluate that expert's competence, independence and the adequacy of their work before relying on it. Supervision and workpaper review by a senior auditor are part of quality.",
      "Independence must be protected throughout. Auditors can advise, but should not make management decisions, design or operate controls, or approve transactions in the area they are auditing. If scope limits are imposed, such as being refused access to a system, the auditor documents it and reports it to audit management and, if needed, the audit committee."
    ],
    terms: [["Audit program", "A step-by-step list of audit procedures designed to meet the objectives of a specific engagement."], ["Workpapers", "The documented record of audit planning, procedures, evidence and conclusions."], ["Engagement letter", "A document that confirms the scope, objectives, timing, responsibilities and access for an audit."], ["Scope limitation", "A restriction on the auditor's access or procedures that prevents gathering sufficient evidence."]],
    example: "During an audit of a trading platform, the business head refuses access to the order database, saying it is too sensitive. The auditor documents the refusal, explains the impact on the audit objective, and escalates to the chief audit executive, who raises it with the audit committee.",
    tip: "If an auditee refuses access or restricts scope, the right answer is to document it and escalate through audit management, not to drop the objective or proceed as if nothing happened.",
    check: [["What must an auditor check before relying on an outside expert's work?", "The expert's competence, independence and objectivity, and whether the work is adequate for the audit objective."], ["What is the purpose of workpapers?", "To document the planning, procedures, evidence and conclusions so that the work can be reviewed and the conclusions supported."]] },
  { t: "Audit testing and sampling: compliance vs substantive tests, statistical and non-statistical sampling",
    body: [
      "Auditors rarely test every item, so they need to know which kind of test answers their question and how to pick a sample they can defend. The exam tests both ideas regularly.",
      "A compliance test (also called a test of controls) checks whether a control is operating as designed, such as whether sampled changes had approval before deployment. A substantive test checks the data itself for errors or misstatement, such as recalculating interest or confirming balances. The two are linked: when compliance testing shows controls are strong, less substantive testing is needed; when controls are weak, the auditor extends substantive testing to find out whether errors actually occurred.",
      "Sampling can be statistical or non-statistical. Statistical sampling uses random or systematic selection and probability math so the auditor can state confidence and project results to the population. Non-statistical (judgmental) sampling relies on auditor judgment, for example choosing all high-value items; it is faster but cannot measure sampling risk objectively. Attribute sampling estimates a rate of occurrence, which suits compliance testing. Variable sampling estimates a quantity such as a dollar amount, which suits substantive testing; monetary unit sampling is a common form. Stop-or-go sampling lets the auditor stop early if few errors appear, and discovery sampling is used to find at least one instance of a rare event such as fraud.",
      "Key terms appear often. The confidence coefficient is how sure you want to be; higher confidence needs a larger sample. The tolerable error rate is the maximum rate you can accept; a lower tolerable rate needs a larger sample. The expected error rate also matters: expecting more errors requires more samples. Stratification splits a population into groups, such as by amount, so each is sampled appropriately."
    ],
    terms: [["Compliance test", "A test of whether a control operates as designed, such as checking approvals on a sample of changes."], ["Substantive test", "A test of the data or transactions themselves to detect errors or misstatement."], ["Attribute sampling", "A method that estimates the rate at which a characteristic, such as a control failure, occurs in a population."], ["Tolerable error rate", "The maximum rate of deviation the auditor is willing to accept and still rely on the control."]],
    example: "An auditor picks 40 random user-access requests from a year of 3,000 and checks each for manager approval. Two lack approval, giving a 5% deviation rate against a tolerable rate of 3%, so she concludes the control is not reliable and extends substantive testing of actual access rights.",
    tip: "Match the method to the question: attribute sampling for how often a control fails, variable or monetary unit sampling for how much money is wrong, discovery sampling for proving a rare event exists.",
    check: [["What happens to sample size if the auditor wants higher confidence?", "It increases; higher confidence requires a larger sample."], ["If compliance tests show a control is weak, what should the auditor do?", "Increase substantive testing to determine whether errors have actually occurred."]] },
  { t: "Audit evidence collection: sufficiency, reliability, relevance; inquiry, observation, inspection, reperformance",
    body: [
      "Audit conclusions are only as good as the evidence behind them. ISACA's standards require evidence that is sufficient, reliable and relevant to support the auditor's opinion, and many exam questions ask which evidence is best.",
      "Sufficient means there is enough of it, for example an adequate sample size. Relevant means it actually addresses the audit objective; evidence that a password policy exists is not relevant to whether it is enforced. Reliable depends on the source and method. As a rule, evidence from an independent outside source is more reliable than evidence from the auditee; evidence the auditor obtains directly is more reliable than evidence handed over; documentary evidence beats oral statements; and original documents beat copies. Evidence produced by a system with strong controls is more reliable than evidence from a weakly controlled system.",
      "The main collection techniques each have strengths. Inquiry, meaning interviews and questionnaires, helps understanding but is weak alone and needs corroboration. Observation shows a process at one moment but people may behave differently when watched. Inspection examines documents, configurations and records. Reperformance means the auditor independently executes the control or calculation, which gives strong evidence. Walkthroughs trace one transaction end to end to confirm understanding of the design. Computer-assisted techniques extract and analyze data directly.",
      "All evidence must be documented in workpapers with source, date and how it was obtained, and protected from alteration. When electronic evidence is involved, the auditor should obtain it directly from the system where possible, and consider hashing or read-only copies to show it was not changed."
    ],
    terms: [["Sufficient evidence", "Enough evidence, in quantity, to support the audit conclusion."], ["Reperformance", "The auditor independently executing a control or calculation to confirm it produces the correct result."], ["Corroboration", "Obtaining additional evidence to confirm information gathered through inquiry or other weaker sources."], ["Walkthrough", "Tracing a single transaction through a process to confirm the auditor's understanding of controls."]],
    example: "A system administrator says all servers enforce 12-character passwords. The auditor does not accept this statement alone. She extracts the password policy settings directly from the directory service while the administrator watches and confirms the value is 12 on every domain.",
    tip: "When ranking evidence, prefer independent over internal, auditor-obtained over auditee-provided, and documentary or reperformed over oral statements.",
    check: [["Why is inquiry alone usually not enough?", "Oral statements may be inaccurate or self-serving; they must be corroborated by inspection, observation or reperformance."], ["Which is more reliable: a bank confirmation received directly by the auditor or a bank statement supplied by the client?", "The confirmation received directly from the bank, because it comes from an independent source without passing through the auditee."]] },
  { t: "Audit data analytics and CAATs, continuous auditing and continuous monitoring",
    body: [
      "Modern organizations process millions of transactions, and sampling a few dozen can miss patterns that only appear across the whole population. Data analytics and computer-assisted audit techniques (CAATs) let the auditor test entire populations quickly and precisely.",
      "Generalized audit software and data analysis tools, including spreadsheets, SQL and scripting languages, let an auditor import data and run tests such as finding duplicate payments, gaps in invoice numbers, transactions posted on weekends, vendors sharing bank accounts with employees, or orders just below an approval limit. Other CAATs include test data (running dummy transactions through the live program to check its logic), parallel simulation (the auditor reprocesses real data with their own program and compares results) and the integrated test facility (fictitious entities set up in production so test transactions flow alongside real ones).",
      "Continuous auditing moves testing close to real time. Techniques include embedded audit modules, which flag transactions meeting audit criteria as they are processed, and snapshot or audit hooks that capture data at points in processing. Continuous monitoring, in contrast, is a management responsibility: management uses automated tools to watch its own controls and key indicators. Auditors may rely on well-designed continuous monitoring, but it is still management's control.",
      "Using analytics brings its own controls. The auditor must confirm the data extracted is complete and accurate, for example by reconciling record counts and totals to the source system, must protect sensitive data during analysis, and should have read-only access so the audit cannot change production data. Evaluating automated and decision-making systems, including AI models, also falls here: the auditor asks how their outputs are validated and monitored."
    ],
    terms: [["CAAT", "Computer-assisted audit technique: using software to extract and analyze data or test system logic as part of an audit."], ["Parallel simulation", "Reprocessing production data with auditor-controlled software and comparing the results with the production output."], ["Embedded audit module", "Code inside an application that selects transactions meeting audit criteria as they are processed."], ["Continuous monitoring", "Management's ongoing, automated oversight of controls and key indicators."]],
    example: "Using a year of accounts payable data, an auditor joins the vendor master file with the employee file on bank account number and finds two vendors paid into employees' accounts. She reconciles her extract to the ledger totals first to prove the data was complete.",
    tip: "Before trusting any analytics result, verify the completeness and accuracy of the extracted data. And remember that continuous monitoring belongs to management, while continuous auditing belongs to audit.",
    check: [["What is the main advantage of data analytics over sampling?", "It can test 100% of the population, catching patterns and exceptions that a sample would likely miss."], ["What should the auditor do before analyzing an extract?", "Confirm it is complete and accurate by reconciling counts and totals to the source system."]] },
  { t: "Audit reporting, follow-up and quality assurance of the audit function",
    body: [
      "An audit only changes things when its results are communicated clearly and acted on. Reporting, follow-up and quality assurance close the loop and keep the audit function credible.",
      "A well-written finding has five parts: condition (what the auditor found), criteria (what should be, such as a policy or standard), cause (why the gap exists), effect (the risk or impact) and recommendation. Findings are discussed with the auditee before the report is final, usually at an exit meeting, to confirm facts and obtain management responses that name corrective actions, owners and dates. If management disagrees, the auditor rechecks the evidence; if the finding still stands, it stays in the report along with management's view. The report states scope, objectives, period covered, an overall conclusion and any scope limitations, and it is distributed to the audit committee and appropriate management.",
      "Follow-up verifies that agreed actions were actually implemented and effective. The auditor tracks open findings and tests the fixes; management's statement that something is done is not enough. If management chooses to accept a risk the auditor considers unacceptable, the auditor discusses it with senior management and, if unresolved, the audit committee. The auditor never implements the fix, because that would impair independence.",
      "Quality assurance and improvement cover the audit function itself. They include supervision and review of workpapers, ongoing internal monitoring, periodic self-assessments and external quality assessments against standards, and feedback from auditees. Metrics such as plan completion, time to report and percentage of recommendations implemented help the function improve."
    ],
    terms: [["Condition", "The part of a finding that states what the auditor actually observed."], ["Criteria", "The standard, policy or expectation that the condition is measured against."], ["Exit meeting", "A meeting at the end of fieldwork where findings are discussed with management before the report is finalized."], ["Follow-up", "Audit procedures that verify whether agreed corrective actions were implemented and effective."]],
    example: "An audit finds that firewall changes are not reviewed. Management agrees to introduce monthly reviews by the network manager by March. In April the auditor samples three monthly reviews, confirms they were performed and documented, and closes the finding.",
    tip: "Auditors report and verify; they do not fix. Any option where the auditor implements the corrective action or quietly drops a supported finding is wrong.",
    check: [["What should an auditor do if management disagrees with a finding?", "Re-examine the evidence; if the finding is still supported, report it along with management's response."], ["Name two activities in an audit quality assurance program.", "Any two of: workpaper supervision and review, internal self-assessments, external quality assessments, auditee feedback surveys and performance metrics."]] },
  { t: "Laws, regulations and industry standards affecting the organization",
    body: [
      "Organizations operate under a web of legal and contractual obligations: privacy and data protection laws, financial reporting rules, sector regulations for health or banking, breach notification laws, and industry standards such as the Payment Card Industry Data Security Standard. An IS auditor must understand which of these apply, because they become the criteria against which controls are judged.",
      "The auditor does not need to be a lawyer, but should know how the organization identifies its obligations. A mature organization keeps a register of applicable laws, regulations and contractual requirements, assigns owners, assesses the gap between requirements and current controls, and monitors for changes. Legal counsel and compliance functions usually own this process; IT translates requirements into technical controls such as encryption, retention and logging.",
      "When a new requirement appears, the first step is for management to assess its applicability and impact, then plan and implement controls. Specific measures, such as buying tools or changing contracts, should follow that assessment rather than precede it. Requirements can conflict across countries, for example one law requiring records to be kept for years and another requiring personal data to be deleted when no longer needed. The organization must resolve these conflicts with legal advice and document its decisions.",
      "Industry standards and frameworks, such as ISO/IEC 27001 for information security management or NIST publications, are usually voluntary unless a law or contract makes them mandatory. They are still useful as criteria for audits and as a way to structure controls. For the exam, remember that contracts with customers or card brands can impose obligations as binding in practice as law."
    ],
    terms: [["Regulatory compliance", "Meeting the requirements imposed by laws and regulations that apply to the organization."], ["Compliance register", "A maintained list of applicable legal, regulatory and contractual requirements with owners and status."], ["Breach notification", "A legal requirement to inform regulators or affected people when certain data is compromised."], ["Industry standard", "A requirement set developed by an industry body, such as for card payments, which may become binding through contracts."]],
    example: "A software company expands into a new country with a strict data protection law. Before any technical changes, the compliance team maps the law's requirements against current practices, finds gaps in consent records and data transfer terms, and assigns remediation owners with deadlines.",
    tip: "The first audit question about a new law is whether management has identified and assessed the requirements. Controls come after that assessment.",
    check: [["Who usually owns identifying applicable laws: IT, legal/compliance or audit?", "Legal and compliance functions, with IT implementing technical controls and audit providing assurance."], ["Can a voluntary standard become mandatory?", "Yes, when a law or contract requires it, such as a payment card standard required by agreements with card brands."]] },
  { t: "IT governance, organizational structure and IT strategy: board, steering committee, business alignment",
    body: [
      "IT governance is how an organization makes sure its use of technology supports its goals, delivers value, manages risk and uses resources responsibly. Governance is different from management. Governance sets direction, makes high-level decisions and monitors performance; management plans, builds, runs and monitors activities within that direction.",
      "The board of directors is ultimately accountable for governance, including IT governance. Boards often use committees: an audit committee oversees internal controls and audit, and a board-level IT strategy committee may advise on technology direction. At management level, an IT steering committee of senior business and IT leaders prioritizes projects, approves investments, monitors progress and resolves conflicts over resources. The chief information officer leads IT, and in many organizations a chief information security officer leads security, ideally with enough independence to raise risks without being overruled by IT delivery pressure.",
      "IT strategy should flow from business strategy. A good strategic plan states where the business is going, what IT must deliver to support it, which investments come first and how success will be measured. Short-term operational plans then implement the strategy. Signs of poor alignment include projects approved only by IT, unclear business sponsors, and IT measuring itself only on technical metrics.",
      "Organizational structure matters for control. Reporting lines should avoid conflicts, for example security should not report to the person whose systems it must challenge if that silences concerns. Audit should report functionally to the audit committee. The auditor reviews governance by examining committee charters, minutes, strategy documents and how decisions are actually made."
    ],
    terms: [["IT governance", "The leadership, structures and processes that ensure IT supports the organization's strategy and objectives."], ["IT steering committee", "A management committee of business and IT leaders that prioritizes, approves and monitors IT investments."], ["Business alignment", "The degree to which IT plans and investments support the organization's strategic goals."], ["IT strategy committee", "A board-level committee that advises the board on the strategic direction of IT."]],
    example: "An auditor reviews steering committee minutes for a year and finds that business leaders attended only one of twelve meetings. Projects were effectively chosen by IT alone. She reports a governance weakness: investments may not reflect business priorities.",
    tip: "Governance is the board's job; management executes. If an answer places ultimate accountability with the CIO or IT department, look for the board instead.",
    check: [["What is the difference between governance and management?", "Governance sets direction and monitors; management plans, builds and runs activities within that direction."], ["What evidence shows whether a steering committee is effective?", "Charters, attendance by business leaders, minutes showing real decisions on priorities and investment, and follow-up on project performance."]] },
  { t: "IT policies, standards, procedures and governance frameworks such as COBIT",
    body: [
      "Policies turn governance intentions into rules. A clear document hierarchy lets everyone know what is required and lets auditors test against something concrete.",
      "At the top, a policy is a high-level statement of management intent, approved by senior management, such as requiring all information to be protected according to its classification. Standards make policies specific and mandatory, for example requiring a particular encryption strength. Procedures give step-by-step instructions to carry out a task. Guidelines are recommended but optional practices. Baselines define minimum configurations for a particular platform. Policies should be reviewed regularly, at least annually or after significant change, communicated to staff, and enforced. An auditor looks for approval, currency, communication and evidence that practice matches the written word.",
      "Governance frameworks give structure. COBIT, developed by ISACA, is a framework for the governance and management of enterprise information and technology. It separates governance objectives (evaluate, direct and monitor) from management objectives (align, plan and organize; build, acquire and implement; deliver, service and support; monitor, evaluate and assess). It uses design factors to tailor a governance system to each organization and capability levels to measure processes. Other frameworks complement it: ITIL for IT service management, ISO/IEC 27001 for information security management systems, and the NIST Cybersecurity Framework for organizing cybersecurity outcomes.",
      "A policy that does not reflect reality is a risk, and so is a reality that has no policy. The auditor's job is to compare them, report gaps and check that exceptions are formally approved and tracked."
    ],
    terms: [["Policy", "A high-level, management-approved statement of intent and direction."], ["Standard", "A mandatory, specific requirement that supports a policy."], ["Procedure", "Detailed, step-by-step instructions to perform a task."], ["COBIT", "ISACA's framework for governance and management of enterprise information and technology."]],
    example: "An organization's policy requires quarterly access reviews, but the procedure document has not been updated in four years and staff follow an informal process. The auditor reports the gap and recommends updating the procedure and evidencing each review.",
    tip: "Policies are approved by senior management and state intent; they should not contain technical detail. If a question asks what the auditor reviews first to understand management's intent, pick the policy.",
    check: [["Which document type is optional?", "A guideline; policies, standards and procedures are mandatory."], ["What does COBIT separate governance objectives from?", "Management objectives; governance evaluates, directs and monitors, while management plans, builds, runs and monitors."]] },
  { t: "Enterprise architecture and enterprise risk management (ERM)",
    body: [
      "Enterprise architecture (EA) is the blueprint of how an organization's business processes, information, applications and technology fit together, both today and in the target future state. It helps leaders see duplication, gaps and dependencies so that investments move the organization toward a coherent design instead of adding disconnected systems.",
      "EA frameworks such as TOGAF or the Zachman Framework describe the enterprise from several views: business, data, application and technology. A typical EA effort documents the current state, defines the target state, and produces a roadmap of projects to close the gap. For auditors, EA matters because well-governed architecture reduces complexity, which reduces risk, and because architecture decisions, such as which systems hold sensitive data and how they connect, shape where controls are needed.",
      "Enterprise risk management (ERM) is the organization-wide process of identifying, assessing, responding to and monitoring risks to objectives. IT risk is one category within ERM, alongside financial, operational, strategic and compliance risk. Key ideas include risk appetite (how much risk the organization is willing to accept to achieve its goals), risk tolerance (acceptable variation around objectives), risk owners, risk registers, and the four responses: mitigate, transfer, avoid or accept. Residual risk remains after controls and must be within appetite or formally accepted by an owner with authority.",
      "The auditor evaluates whether IT risks are identified and assessed consistently with ERM, whether owners are assigned, whether responses are implemented and whether reporting reaches the board. The auditor does not own or accept risks."
    ],
    terms: [["Enterprise architecture", "A structured description of business processes, information, applications and technology and how they should evolve."], ["Risk appetite", "The amount and type of risk an organization is willing to pursue or retain to achieve its objectives."], ["Residual risk", "The risk remaining after controls and other responses are applied."], ["Risk owner", "The person accountable for managing a particular risk and deciding on its response."]],
    example: "A merger leaves a company with three customer databases. The enterprise architecture team defines a target state with one customer master, and the ERM register records data inconsistency and privacy exposure as risks owned by the chief operating officer until consolidation is done.",
    tip: "Risk acceptance belongs to a business owner with authority, within the risk appetite. Auditors identify and report risks; they never accept them.",
    check: [["Name the four risk responses.", "Mitigate (reduce), transfer (share), avoid and accept."], ["How does enterprise architecture reduce risk?", "By reducing complexity and duplication and making dependencies visible, so controls can be placed where they are needed."]] },
  { t: "Privacy programs and privacy principles",
    body: [
      "Privacy is about the proper handling of personal information: how it is collected, used, shared, retained and disposed of, and what rights individuals have over it. Security protects data; privacy decides what the organization should do with it in the first place. A privacy program brings these decisions under governance.",
      "Widely accepted privacy principles, reflected in laws and frameworks around the world, include: lawful basis and transparency (people are told what is collected and why); purpose limitation (data is used only for the purposes stated); data minimization (collect only what is needed); accuracy; storage limitation (keep it no longer than necessary); integrity and confidentiality (protect it); individual rights such as access, correction and deletion; and accountability (the organization can demonstrate compliance).",
      "A privacy program typically has an accountable leader such as a privacy officer or data protection officer, a privacy policy and notices, an inventory of personal data and where it flows, privacy impact assessments for new systems and changes, procedures for responding to individuals' requests, contracts that bind processors, breach response procedures and training. Privacy by design means building these considerations into systems from the start rather than adding them later.",
      "Auditors review whether the program exists and works: is there a data inventory, are impact assessments done before launch, are requests handled on time, are retention schedules applied, and are third parties bound by appropriate terms? Reusing data for a new purpose, collecting more than needed, and keeping data forever are common findings."
    ],
    terms: [["Purpose limitation", "The principle that personal data is used only for the specific purposes for which it was collected."], ["Data minimization", "Collecting and keeping only the personal data that is necessary for the stated purpose."], ["Privacy impact assessment (PIA)", "An evaluation of how a new system or change affects personal data and privacy risk, done before it is implemented."], ["Privacy by design", "Building privacy protections into systems and processes from the start."]],
    example: "A marketing team wants to analyze purchase histories to target ads. The privacy officer runs an impact assessment, finds that customers were told data would be used only for fulfilment, and requires consent before the new use begins.",
    tip: "Security and privacy overlap but differ. A question about using data for a new purpose or collecting too much is about privacy principles, not encryption.",
    check: [["What is the purpose of a privacy impact assessment?", "To identify and address privacy risks of a new system or change before it goes live."], ["Which principle limits how long personal data is kept?", "Storage limitation (retention only as long as necessary)."]] },
  { t: "Data governance and data classification",
    body: [
      "Data governance defines who is accountable for data, how its quality and protection are managed, and how decisions about it are made. Without it, nobody owns data quality, sensitive records spread across uncontrolled copies, and controls are applied inconsistently.",
      "Key roles recur on the exam. The data owner is a business manager accountable for a set of data; they decide its classification, approve who may access it and set requirements for retention. The data custodian, often IT, implements and operates the controls the owner specifies, such as backups and access settings. Data stewards look after data quality and definitions day to day. Users access data according to their authorized role. Many organizations also have a data governance council that sets policy and resolves disputes.",
      "Data classification assigns data to categories, for example public, internal, confidential and restricted, based on its sensitivity and value. Each level has handling rules for storage, transmission, sharing, labeling and disposal. Classification lets protection be proportionate: expensive controls go where they matter most. It also underpins data loss prevention rules, encryption requirements and access decisions. Classification should be reviewed over time, because data can become more or less sensitive.",
      "Data quality is part of governance too. Accurate, complete, consistent and timely data supports reliable decisions and reports. Auditors review whether owners are assigned, whether a data inventory exists, whether classification is applied and matched by controls, and whether quality issues are measured and fixed."
    ],
    terms: [["Data owner", "The business manager accountable for a data set, who decides its classification and approves access."], ["Data custodian", "The party, often IT, that implements and maintains controls over data on the owner's behalf."], ["Data classification", "Assigning data to sensitivity levels that determine how it must be handled and protected."], ["Data steward", "A person responsible for the quality, definitions and proper use of data day to day."]],
    example: "An auditor finds that a customer database has no named owner, so the DBA grants access to anyone who asks. She recommends assigning the head of customer service as owner, classifying the data as confidential and requiring the owner's approval for all access.",
    tip: "The owner decides and is accountable; the custodian implements. When asked who classifies data or approves access, choose the business owner, not IT or security.",
    check: [["Who approves access to a data set?", "The data owner."], ["Why classify data?", "So protection and handling are proportionate to the data's sensitivity and value, and so controls are applied consistently."]] },
  { t: "IT resource management: segregation of duties, staffing, budgeting and portfolio management",
    body: [
      "IT resources include people, money, infrastructure and applications. Managing them well means having the right skills, spending wisely, choosing the right investments and organizing duties so no single person can cause and hide a serious error or fraud.",
      "Segregation of duties (SoD) separates incompatible functions: requesting, approving, recording and custody of assets, or in IT terms developing code, approving changes and moving code to production. Other conflicts include security administration combined with system administration, or a database administrator also approving their own access. Where full SoD is not possible, such as in a small team, compensating controls like independent log review, supervisory approval and reconciliations reduce the risk. Auditors often build an SoD matrix to find conflicts.",
      "People controls include background checks during hiring, clear job descriptions, training, mandatory vacations and job rotation (which can uncover fraud), succession planning to avoid key-person dependency, and prompt removal of access on termination. When contractors or temporary staff are used, the same controls must apply.",
      "Financial and portfolio management ensure IT spending delivers value. IT budgets should link to the strategic plan, and actual spending should be tracked against budget. Portfolio management evaluates all proposed and running investments together, ranking them by value, risk and alignment and deciding which to fund, continue or stop. Chargeback or showback models help business units see the cost of IT they consume. Auditors check whether investment decisions are made on documented business cases and reviewed over time."
    ],
    terms: [["Segregation of duties", "Dividing incompatible duties among different people so that no single person can commit and conceal errors or fraud."], ["Compensating control", "An alternative control that reduces risk when the ideal control, such as full segregation of duties, cannot be applied."], ["Portfolio management", "Managing a set of IT investments together to maximize value and alignment within available resources."], ["Job rotation", "Periodically moving staff between roles, which reduces dependence on individuals and can reveal irregularities."]],
    example: "In a five-person IT team, one engineer both writes code and deploys it. The auditor recommends that the IT manager review a weekly report of all production changes against approved tickets, and that deployment logs be sent to a system the engineer cannot edit.",
    tip: "The classic IT SoD conflict is the same person developing and moving code to production. If full separation is impossible, look for a compensating detective control done by someone independent.",
    check: [["Give two examples of incompatible IT duties.", "Developing code and migrating it to production; administering security and approving one's own access; entering transactions and approving them."], ["Why do mandatory vacations help detect fraud?", "Someone else performs the duties while the employee is away and may notice irregularities that required ongoing concealment."]] },
  { t: "IT vendor management: outsourcing, contracts, right to audit and SOC reports",
    body: [
      "Organizations increasingly rely on vendors for software, cloud services, managed operations and outsourced processes. Outsourcing moves the work but not the accountability: if a payroll provider leaks employee data, the employer still answers to its staff and regulators. Vendor management is how the organization keeps control of risks it no longer directly operates.",
      "The vendor lifecycle starts with planning and due diligence: defining requirements, assessing the vendor's financial stability, security posture, controls, location and subcontractors. The contract then sets expectations: scope, service levels with measurable targets and remedies, security and privacy requirements, data ownership and location, breach notification timelines, right to audit or access to independent assurance, subcontracting limits, business continuity commitments, and exit terms including data return and deletion. Source code escrow can protect the customer if a software vendor fails.",
      "During the relationship, the organization monitors performance against service levels, reviews security reports, tracks issues and reassesses risk periodically. Service Organization Control (SOC) reports are a common assurance source. SOC 1 covers controls relevant to customers' financial reporting. SOC 2 covers the trust services criteria of security, availability, processing integrity, confidentiality and privacy. Type 1 reports on design at a point in time; Type 2 also tests operating effectiveness over a period. Readers must check the period, scope, exceptions and complementary user entity controls, which are controls the customer must operate itself.",
      "At the end, the exit plan ensures a smooth transition and that the vendor returns or securely destroys data."
    ],
    terms: [["Right-to-audit clause", "A contract term allowing the customer, or its auditors, to audit the vendor's controls."], ["SOC 2 Type 2", "An independent report on a service organization's controls over the trust services criteria, including testing of operating effectiveness over a period."], ["Complementary user entity controls", "Controls that the customer must perform for the service organization's controls to be effective."], ["Source code escrow", "An arrangement where a third party holds a vendor's source code, released to the customer if the vendor fails."]],
    example: "An auditor reviews a cloud HR vendor's SOC 2 Type 2 report. It covers the right service and period, but lists an exception in access reviews and requires customers to manage their own user provisioning. She checks that the company does that provisioning control and follows up on the vendor exception.",
    tip: "Type 1 means design only at one point; Type 2 adds operating effectiveness over time. An auditor relying on a vendor's controls normally wants Type 2, and must still check complementary user entity controls.",
    check: [["Does outsourcing transfer accountability?", "No. It transfers the work; the organization remains accountable for its data and obligations."], ["What does a SOC 1 report focus on?", "Controls at a service organization that are relevant to its customers' financial reporting."]] },
  { t: "IT performance monitoring, reporting and quality management: KPIs, balanced scorecard, maturity",
    body: [
      "Governance needs feedback. Leaders cannot direct IT well unless they know whether it is delivering what was promised, at what cost and with what risk. Performance monitoring provides that feedback through meaningful measures and regular reporting.",
      "Key performance indicators (KPIs) measure how well a process is achieving its goals, such as percentage of changes causing incidents or system availability against target. Key risk indicators (KRIs) signal rising risk, such as the number of unpatched critical systems. Good metrics are linked to objectives, measurable from reliable data, and reported to people who can act on them. The IT balanced scorecard organizes measures into four perspectives: financial or business contribution, customer or user orientation, internal processes (operational excellence) and learning and growth (future orientation). It prevents IT from judging itself only on technical metrics.",
      "Quality management ensures IT processes and products meet requirements consistently. Quality assurance defines and checks processes, while quality control inspects outputs. Standards such as ISO 9001 for quality management and ISO/IEC 20000 for IT service management provide structure. Capability or maturity models, such as the capability levels used in COBIT, rate processes from incomplete or ad hoc to optimized, helping organizations identify gaps between current and target capability and plan improvement.",
      "Auditors check whether metrics are defined, accurate and reported to the right level, whether targets are realistic, and whether poor results trigger action. A common finding is a dashboard full of activity counts, such as tickets closed, that says little about business value or risk."
    ],
    terms: [["Key performance indicator (KPI)", "A measure of how well a process or activity is achieving its objective."], ["Key risk indicator (KRI)", "A metric that signals increasing exposure to a particular risk."], ["Balanced scorecard", "A performance tool that measures across financial, customer, internal process and learning and growth perspectives."], ["Capability level", "A rating of how well a process is performed and managed, from incomplete to optimized."]],
    example: "A CIO's monthly report lists servers managed and help desk tickets closed. The auditor recommends adding measures tied to business goals, such as availability of revenue-critical systems, project benefits achieved and user satisfaction, arranged as a balanced scorecard.",
    tip: "Look for measures that show value and outcomes, not just activity. A balanced scorecard that links IT to business goals is usually the best answer for showing IT's contribution.",
    check: [["What is the difference between a KPI and a KRI?", "A KPI measures performance toward a goal; a KRI signals increasing risk exposure."], ["What are the four balanced scorecard perspectives?", "Financial (business contribution), customer (user orientation), internal process and learning and growth."]] },
  { t: "Project governance and management: roles, steering, earned value and project risk",
    body: [
      "Many IT failures are project failures: systems delivered late, over budget, missing key controls or not delivering the promised benefits. Project governance makes sure projects are justified, directed and monitored by the right people, and the auditor checks that it works.",
      "Key roles include the project sponsor, a senior business leader who owns the business case and provides funding and direction; the project steering committee, which makes major decisions and approves changes in scope, budget or schedule; the project manager, who runs day-to-day work; the project management office (PMO), which sets standards and tracks the portfolio; and users and system owners, who define requirements and accept the result. Quality assurance and security staff advise on standards and controls.",
      "Monitoring needs objective measures. Earned value analysis compares planned value (the budgeted cost of work scheduled), earned value (the budgeted cost of work actually completed) and actual cost. Cost variance is earned value minus actual cost; schedule variance is earned value minus planned value; negative values mean over budget or behind schedule. The cost performance index (EV divided by AC) and schedule performance index (EV divided by PV) show efficiency, where values below 1 are unfavorable. Other tools include the critical path method, Gantt charts and PERT estimates.",
      "Project risk management identifies risks early, such as unclear requirements, scope creep, key staff leaving or vendor delays, and tracks responses. Scope changes should go through formal change control with impact analysis and steering committee approval. The auditor reviews whether the project has a clear sponsor, realistic plans, honest status reporting, controlled changes and an agreed definition of done."
    ],
    terms: [["Project sponsor", "The senior business leader who owns the business case, funds the project and is accountable for its benefits."], ["Earned value", "The budgeted cost of the work actually completed at a point in time."], ["Cost performance index (CPI)", "Earned value divided by actual cost; below 1 means the project is over budget."], ["Scope creep", "Uncontrolled growth in project scope without matching changes in budget, time or approval."]],
    example: "A CRM project reports green status every month. The auditor calculates that earned value is $300,000 against an actual cost of $450,000, giving a CPI of 0.67. She reports that status reporting is not reflecting reality and that the steering committee needs a revised forecast.",
    tip: "Know the formulas: CV = EV - AC, SV = EV - PV, CPI = EV / AC, SPI = EV / PV. Negative variances or indexes below 1 mean trouble.",
    check: [["Who owns the business case for a project?", "The project sponsor, a senior business leader."], ["A project has EV of 80 and PV of 100. Is it ahead of or behind schedule?", "Behind schedule: SV is -20 and SPI is 0.8."]] },
  { t: "Business case and feasibility analysis",
    body: [
      "Before an organization spends money on a new system, it should understand why. The business case documents the problem or opportunity, the options considered, the costs, benefits and risks of each, and the recommended solution. It is the reference point for deciding whether to start, continue or stop a project.",
      "Feasibility analysis examines whether a solution is practical from several angles. Technical feasibility asks whether the technology exists and fits the environment. Economic feasibility compares costs with benefits using measures such as return on investment, net present value, payback period and total cost of ownership, which includes ongoing support, licenses and training, not only purchase price. Operational feasibility asks whether people and processes can adopt it. Legal and compliance feasibility checks regulatory constraints. Schedule feasibility considers whether it can be delivered when needed.",
      "Options usually include doing nothing, improving the current system, buying a commercial product, using a cloud service or building a custom system. A buy decision leads to a structured selection: defining requirements, issuing a request for proposal, evaluating vendors against weighted criteria, checking references and financial stability, and negotiating contracts. A build decision leads into a development methodology.",
      "The business case is not a one-time document. It should be revisited at major milestones and whenever costs, benefits or risks change significantly. If a project's justification no longer holds, stopping it is a legitimate outcome; money already spent is a sunk cost and should not by itself justify continuing. After implementation, the post-implementation review checks whether the promised benefits were realized."
    ],
    terms: [["Business case", "A document that justifies an investment by comparing options, costs, benefits and risks."], ["Total cost of ownership (TCO)", "All costs of a solution over its life, including acquisition, operation, support and retirement."], ["Feasibility study", "An analysis of whether a proposed solution is technically, economically, operationally and legally practical."], ["Sunk cost", "Money already spent that cannot be recovered and should not drive future decisions."]],
    example: "A hospital compares upgrading its old scheduling system, buying a commercial product and subscribing to a cloud service. The business case shows the cloud option has the lowest five-year total cost of ownership and meets privacy requirements, so the steering committee approves it with a review at the end of the pilot.",
    tip: "When a project's costs rise or benefits fall, the best answer is usually to re-evaluate the business case, not to continue because of money already spent.",
    check: [["Name three types of feasibility.", "Any three of: technical, economic, operational, legal or compliance, and schedule."], ["When should a business case be reviewed after approval?", "At key milestones, when significant changes occur, and in the post-implementation review."]] },
  { t: "System development methodologies: SDLC, agile, DevOps, prototyping and RAD",
    body: [
      "A system development methodology is the structured way an organization turns requirements into working software. Auditors do not need to prefer one method, but they must understand where controls sit in each and what evidence to expect.",
      "The traditional system development life cycle (SDLC), or waterfall model, moves through phases in sequence: feasibility, requirements definition, design, development, testing, implementation and post-implementation. Each phase ends with deliverables and sign-off, which makes control points easy to identify. Its weakness is inflexibility: requirements are fixed early, and problems found late are expensive to fix.",
      "Agile methods, such as Scrum, deliver working software in short iterations called sprints. Requirements are captured as user stories in a prioritized product backlog, each with acceptance criteria. Roles include the product owner, who prioritizes the backlog; the development team; and a facilitator such as a Scrum master. Documentation is lighter but still exists. Auditors look for security and control requirements in the backlog, definition of done criteria, testing within each sprint, and controlled release. DevOps extends agile by joining development and operations with automated build, test and deployment pipelines. Controls then become automated gates: code review, automated security tests and approval before production deployment. DevSecOps emphasizes building security into these pipelines.",
      "Prototyping builds quick models to clarify requirements with users; the risk is that a prototype is pushed into production without proper controls. Rapid application development (RAD) uses prototypes, reusable components and time-boxing to deliver fast. Whatever the method, the auditor checks that requirements include controls, that testing is adequate, and that changes to production are authorized."
    ],
    terms: [["Waterfall (SDLC)", "A sequential development approach where each phase is completed and signed off before the next begins."], ["Product backlog", "A prioritized list of features and requirements, usually written as user stories, used in agile development."], ["DevOps", "Practices that combine development and operations with automation to deliver changes quickly and reliably."], ["Prototyping", "Building an early working model of a system to refine requirements with users."]],
    example: "An auditor reviews a team using Scrum and DevOps. She finds user stories for security requirements in the backlog, automated tests in the pipeline and a rule that the pipeline blocks deployment without a peer-approved pull request, which gives evidence of change control without heavy documents.",
    tip: "Agile and DevOps do not mean no controls. Look for controls in different places: backlog acceptance criteria, definition of done, automated tests and pipeline approval gates.",
    check: [["What is a major risk of prototyping?", "The prototype may be moved into production without proper controls, testing or documentation."], ["Where are change controls typically found in a DevOps environment?", "In automated pipeline gates such as required code reviews, automated tests and approvals before deployment."]] },
  { t: "Control identification and design: input, processing and output application controls",
    body: [
      "Controls are cheapest and most effective when designed into a system rather than added after go-live. During requirements and design, the project team, with advice from security, risk and audit, should identify the risks in the business process and build in application controls to address them. The auditor can advise on what controls are needed but should not design them, to protect independence.",
      "Input controls ensure data entering the system is authorized, complete and accurate. Examples include authorization of source transactions, edit and validation checks such as validity checks (the value is allowed), range checks, limit checks, reasonableness checks, format checks, existence checks against master data, check digits on account numbers, and completeness checks for required fields. Batch controls such as record counts, control totals and hash totals help confirm that a batch was entered completely.",
      "Processing controls ensure data is processed completely and accurately. Examples include run-to-run totals that carry control figures between processing steps, recalculation and reasonableness checks, exception reports for items that fail rules, and controls to prevent duplicate processing. Data file controls protect master data from unauthorized change, for example by logging and reviewing changes to vendor bank details.",
      "Output controls ensure results are complete, accurate and delivered only to authorized recipients. They include reconciling output totals with input and processing totals, controlled distribution of sensitive reports, retention rules and secure disposal. Audit trails that record who did what and when support both detection and investigation. The auditor checks that controls match the risks, are tested, and cannot be bypassed."
    ],
    terms: [["Validity check", "An edit check that accepts only values that are permitted for a field, such as real dates or valid codes."], ["Hash total", "A total of a non-financial field, such as account numbers, used to detect changes or missing items in a batch."], ["Run-to-run totals", "Control totals carried from one processing step to the next to confirm nothing was lost or added."], ["Check digit", "A calculated digit appended to a number that detects transcription errors when the number is entered."]],
    example: "In a new expense system, the design team adds a limit check that flags claims over $5,000 for director approval, a validity check that rejects future dates, a duplicate check on receipt numbers and a monthly reconciliation of paid claims to the general ledger.",
    tip: "Match the control to the error: validity or range checks for bad individual values, record counts and hash totals for missing or altered batch items, reconciliations for completeness of output.",
    check: [["Which control detects a transposed digit in an account number?", "A check digit."], ["Why should controls be designed in rather than added later?", "It is cheaper and more effective, and it avoids gaps between the system and the controls built around it."]] },
  { t: "System readiness and implementation testing: unit, integration, system, UAT and regression",
    body: [
      "Testing shows whether a system does what it should, including its controls, before it is trusted with real work. A system that goes live untested can corrupt data, expose information or stop business operations.",
      "Testing happens at several levels. Unit testing checks individual modules, usually by developers. Integration testing checks that modules and interfaces work together and pass data correctly. System testing checks the whole system against functional and non-functional requirements, including performance, stress, security, recovery and usability. User acceptance testing (UAT) lets business users confirm the system meets their requirements in realistic scenarios; the system owner signs off. Regression testing reruns earlier tests after changes to confirm nothing that previously worked has broken. Final acceptance may also include quality assurance review and security testing, such as vulnerability scans or penetration tests.",
      "Testing techniques include black-box testing, which checks outputs for given inputs without looking at code, and white-box testing, which examines internal logic and paths. Test data should cover valid, invalid and boundary cases, and should not use real personal data unless it is masked or anonymized. Tests should run in a separate test environment that mirrors production, so tests cannot damage live data.",
      "Readiness also covers operations: documentation, trained users and support staff, backup and recovery procedures, monitoring, and a rollback plan. The auditor reviews test plans, results, defect logs and sign-offs, and checks that serious defects were fixed or formally accepted by the owner before go-live."
    ],
    terms: [["Unit testing", "Testing individual program modules in isolation, usually by developers."], ["User acceptance testing (UAT)", "Testing by business users to confirm the system meets their requirements before go-live."], ["Regression testing", "Rerunning previous tests after a change to confirm existing functions still work."], ["Black-box testing", "Testing functionality by checking outputs for given inputs without examining internal code."]],
    example: "Before launching a new billing system, users run UAT scripts covering normal bills, credits and edge cases. Two defects remain open; the finance director signs an acceptance that one is minor and requires the other, which misstates tax, to be fixed and retested before go-live.",
    tip: "UAT sign-off belongs to the business users and system owner, not IT. And production data used in testing should be masked to protect privacy.",
    check: [["What is the purpose of regression testing?", "To confirm that changes have not broken functions that previously worked."], ["Why should testing use a separate environment?", "So tests cannot damage live data or disrupt operations, and so test activity is kept apart from production."]] },
  { t: "Implementation configuration and release management; changeover approaches",
    body: [
      "Going live is one of the riskiest moments in a system's life. Implementation planning, configuration management and release management reduce the chance that the wrong version, a bad setting or an untested change reaches production.",
      "Configuration management records the approved configuration of each component, such as software versions, settings and dependencies, usually in a configuration management database. Version control tracks every change to code and configuration files. Release management packages tested changes into releases, schedules them, and moves them to production through a controlled process with approval, communication and rollback plans. Segregation of duties matters here: developers should not move their own code into production, and production libraries should be protected from direct changes.",
      "Changeover, or cutover, approaches differ in risk and cost. Parallel changeover runs old and new systems together and compares results; it is the safest but most expensive. Phased changeover introduces the new system module by module or site by site, spreading risk. Pilot changeover runs the new system in one location or group first, then rolls out. Direct or abrupt changeover (big bang) switches everything at once; it is the cheapest and quickest but the riskiest, because there is no easy fallback.",
      "Whatever the approach, the organization should have go/no-go criteria, a rollback plan, verified data conversion, trained users and support ready. The auditor checks that the release was authorized, the version deployed matches the tested version, and fallback arrangements existed."
    ],
    terms: [["Release management", "The process of planning, packaging, approving and deploying tested changes into production."], ["Parallel changeover", "Running the old and new systems at the same time and comparing results before switching fully."], ["Direct cutover", "Switching from the old system to the new one at a single point in time with no parallel running."], ["Rollback plan", "A prepared procedure to return to the previous state if a release fails."]],
    example: "A utility company introduces new billing software region by region over six months. Each region goes live only after the previous one's first billing cycle reconciles, and a rollback plan lets any region return to the old system within a day.",
    tip: "Parallel is lowest risk and highest cost; direct cutover is highest risk and lowest cost. Phased and pilot sit in between.",
    check: [["Which changeover approach provides the best fallback?", "Parallel changeover, because the old system keeps running and results can be compared."], ["Why confirm that the version deployed matches the version tested?", "To make sure untested or unauthorized code did not reach production."]] },
  { t: "System migration, infrastructure deployment and data conversion",
    body: [
      "Moving to a new system usually means moving data and often infrastructure too, for example from on-premises servers to the cloud. Migrations fail when data is lost, altered or mismapped, or when the new environment is not configured securely.",
      "Data conversion follows a planned sequence: identify source data and owners, clean it, map fields from old to new formats, define conversion rules, run trial conversions, reconcile results, fix exceptions, and perform the final conversion. Reconciliation is the key control. Record counts, control totals of amounts and hash totals of key fields are compared between source and target, and samples of records are checked in detail. The data owner signs off that the converted data is complete and accurate. The old data and system should be kept, in read-only form, until the new system is verified and any retention requirements are met.",
      "Controls during conversion include restricting who can run conversion programs, logging all manual corrections, keeping an audit trail of conversion runs and protecting sensitive data in transit and in staging areas. Developers should not change production data directly to fix errors.",
      "Infrastructure deployment needs its own controls: approved build standards and hardened configurations, infrastructure as code reviewed like application code, network segmentation, backup and monitoring set up before go-live, and capacity sized for expected load. In cloud migrations, the organization must understand the shared responsibility model and configure identity, encryption and logging properly. The auditor checks conversion plans and reconciliation evidence, sign-offs and the security configuration of the new environment."
    ],
    terms: [["Data conversion", "Transforming and moving data from an old system's format to a new system's format."], ["Data mapping", "Defining how each field in the source system corresponds to fields in the target system."], ["Control total", "A sum of a numeric field, such as amounts, used to confirm that all data was processed or transferred."], ["Infrastructure as code", "Defining and deploying infrastructure through version-controlled configuration files instead of manual setup."]],
    example: "A credit union migrates 200,000 member accounts. Record counts match, but the sum of balances differs by $1,240. Investigation finds that accounts with negative balances were mapped incorrectly; the mapping is fixed, the trial rerun and reconciled, and the finance director signs off.",
    tip: "Reconciliation with record counts, control totals and hash totals, signed off by the data owner, is the key data conversion control. Keep the old data until the conversion is verified.",
    check: [["Who should sign off converted data?", "The data owner in the business, after reviewing reconciliation results."], ["Why should old data be retained after conversion?", "To allow verification, correction or rollback and to meet retention requirements."]] },
  { t: "Post-implementation review and benefits realization",
    body: [
      "A project is not truly finished when the system goes live. The post-implementation review (PIR) asks whether the project achieved what it set out to do and what the organization can learn. It closes the loop with the business case.",
      "A PIR is typically performed after the system has been in production long enough for results to be measured, often several months. It compares actual costs, benefits and schedule with the business case; checks whether user requirements are met and users are satisfied; checks whether controls are working as designed, including security, audit trails and access; reviews outstanding defects and change requests; and captures lessons learned for future projects. It may be performed by the project team, quality assurance or an independent party; an auditor may participate or review it.",
      "Benefits realization is the discipline of planning, tracking and confirming the benefits promised in the business case. Benefits should be defined in measurable terms with owners and target dates, such as reducing processing time by 30% within a year. Benefits often arrive after the project team has disbanded, so business owners must be accountable for delivering them. If benefits are not being realized, the organization should understand why and decide whether further action is needed.",
      "For auditors, the PIR is also a source of evidence about the project's control environment. A missing PIR is itself a finding, because the organization cannot tell whether its investment paid off or learn from mistakes."
    ],
    terms: [["Post-implementation review (PIR)", "A review after go-live that compares actual results with the business case and captures lessons learned."], ["Benefits realization", "Managing and tracking the delivery of the benefits promised by an investment."], ["Lessons learned", "Documented insights from a project that help improve future projects."], ["Benefit owner", "The business person accountable for achieving a specific benefit after implementation."]],
    example: "Six months after launching a self-service portal, the PIR finds that call center volume fell by 18% instead of the promised 30%, because two common requests were not included. The benefit owner adds them to the backlog and the lessons learned note that call data should shape requirements.",
    tip: "The PIR happens after the system has run long enough to measure benefits, not right after go-live and not before. Its main reference point is the business case.",
    check: [["What document does a PIR compare results against?", "The business case, including its costs, benefits and schedule."], ["Who should be accountable for benefits after a project closes?", "Named business benefit owners."]] },
  { t: "IT components and IT asset management: hardware, software, inventory and licensing",
    body: [
      "IT operations rest on many components: servers, storage, network devices, end-user devices, operating systems, databases, middleware and applications, whether on premises or in the cloud. An auditor does not need to configure them, but must understand what each does and what can go wrong, because risks and controls follow from the technology.",
      "IT asset management (ITAM) keeps track of those components through their life: request, procurement, deployment, maintenance, and retirement with secure disposal. The foundation is an accurate inventory recording each asset's owner, location, configuration, criticality, support status and the data it holds. You cannot patch, monitor, license or protect assets you do not know about, so an incomplete inventory weakens nearly every other control. Automated discovery tools help find devices and software that were never recorded.",
      "Software asset management compares installed software with purchased licenses. Using more copies than licensed creates legal and financial exposure, while buying more than needed wastes money. Controls include restricting who can install software, maintaining a list of approved software, periodic license reconciliation and tracking of cloud subscriptions. End-of-life assets that no longer receive vendor updates are a security concern and should be replaced or isolated.",
      "Disposal is part of the lifecycle. Storage media must be sanitized using methods suitable for the media and data sensitivity, such as cryptographic erase or physical destruction, with evidence recorded. The auditor checks that the inventory is complete by comparing it with discovery scans, purchase records and network data, and samples disposals for sanitization evidence."
    ],
    terms: [["IT asset management (ITAM)", "Tracking and managing IT assets through their lifecycle, from acquisition to disposal."], ["Software asset management", "Managing software licenses and installations to ensure compliance and control costs."], ["End of life (EOL)", "The point after which a vendor no longer supports or updates a product."], ["Media sanitization", "Removing data from storage media so that it cannot be recovered."]],
    example: "An auditor compares the asset inventory with a network discovery scan and finds 40 devices on the network that are not in the inventory, including an old file server running an unsupported operating system that nobody was patching.",
    tip: "An accurate asset inventory is the foundation of IT operations and security. When asked what to do first to manage or protect assets, building or verifying the inventory is often the answer.",
    check: [["Why is an incomplete asset inventory a security risk?", "Unknown assets are not patched, monitored or protected, and may be exploited unnoticed."], ["How can an auditor test inventory completeness?", "By comparing it with independent sources such as network discovery scans, purchase records and license data."]] },
  { t: "Job scheduling, production process automation and system interfaces",
    body: [
      "Much IT processing happens without anyone pressing a button. Batch jobs run overnight to post transactions, calculate interest or produce reports, and interfaces move data between systems. If these automated processes fail, run twice or are altered, the damage can be large and quiet.",
      "Job scheduling software runs jobs in the right order at the right time and handles dependencies, such as not running payroll before timesheets load. Controls include restricting who can add or change scheduled jobs, requiring change management for schedule changes, monitoring job completion and failures, reviewing exception and rerun logs, and keeping developers out of production scheduling. Automated scheduling reduces human error compared with manual operation, but only if the schedule itself is protected.",
      "System interfaces move data between applications, in batch files or in real time through application programming interfaces (APIs). Each interface is a point where data can be lost, duplicated, altered or exposed. Controls include record counts and control totals compared at the sending and receiving ends, sequence numbers to detect missing or duplicate transmissions, error handling and exception queues that someone reviews, authentication between systems, encryption in transit and logging. Interfaces should be inventoried with owners, so it is clear who investigates when reconciliation fails.",
      "Production process automation, including robotic process automation (RPA) bots, needs the same discipline: bots should have their own accounts with least privilege, changes to bot logic should go through change control, and their outputs should be monitored. The auditor reviews schedule access, change records, exception handling and interface reconciliations."
    ],
    terms: [["Job scheduler", "Software that runs batch jobs automatically in a defined order and time."], ["System interface", "A connection that transfers data between two applications, by file or in real time."], ["Application programming interface (API)", "A defined way for software components to request and exchange data with each other."], ["Robotic process automation (RPA)", "Software bots that mimic user actions to automate repetitive tasks."]],
    example: "Each night an interface sends approved invoices from procurement to accounts payable. A reconciliation report compares count and total amount at both ends; one morning it shows 412 sent and 409 received, and the AP supervisor investigates the three missing invoices before payments run.",
    tip: "For interfaces, the best completeness control is reconciliation of counts and totals between sending and receiving systems. Encryption protects confidentiality but does not prove completeness.",
    check: [["Why should developers not be able to change the production job schedule?", "They could run or alter jobs in production without change control, bypassing segregation of duties."], ["Name two controls over an interface.", "Any two of: record counts and control totals reconciled at both ends, sequence checks, error queues reviewed by an owner, authentication, encryption and logging."]] },
  { t: "End-user computing and shadow IT",
    body: [
      "End-user computing (EUC) means applications built or managed by business users rather than IT: spreadsheets with complex formulas, desktop databases, reporting tools and low-code apps. EUC is valuable because it is flexible and fast, but it often supports important decisions and financial reports without the controls applied to formal systems.",
      "Typical EUC risks include formula errors, no version control, no testing, no documentation, one person who understands the tool, weak access control, no backup and data copied out of controlled systems. A single wrong cell reference in a spreadsheet used for pricing or financial reporting can cause material errors. A good EUC program inventories critical EUC tools, rates their risk based on use and complexity, and applies proportionate controls: locked formulas, access restriction, version control, independent review of changes, reconciliation to source data and backups.",
      "Shadow IT is technology acquired or used without the IT function's knowledge or approval, most often cloud software subscribed to with a credit card. It can introduce unvetted vendors, unknown data locations, weak authentication, licensing issues and data that is not backed up or covered by retention rules. It usually appears because users have needs IT is not meeting quickly enough.",
      "The best first step is discovery: finding what is in use, for example through expense reports, network traffic analysis or a cloud access security broker, and then assessing risk. Useful services can be brought under governance, risky ones replaced, and policies and approval processes made fast enough that people use them. Blanket bans usually push shadow IT further out of sight."
    ],
    terms: [["End-user computing (EUC)", "Applications and tools developed or managed by business users outside the formal IT function."], ["Shadow IT", "Technology used in the organization without IT's knowledge or approval."], ["EUC inventory", "A list of end-user tools with their owners, purpose and risk rating."], ["Cloud access security broker (CASB)", "A tool that discovers and controls use of cloud services."]],
    example: "Finance calculates quarterly revenue in a spreadsheet with 30 linked tabs maintained by one analyst. The auditor finds no version history or review. The team adds the file to the EUC inventory as high risk, locks formulas, keeps versions and requires a second person to review changes each quarter.",
    tip: "For shadow IT, the best first step is discovery and risk assessment, not blocking everything. For EUC, the key risks are lack of change control, testing, access control and backup.",
    check: [["Why is shadow IT hard to govern?", "IT does not know it exists, so it is not assessed, secured, backed up or covered by contracts and policies."], ["What controls suit a high-risk spreadsheet?", "Inventory and ownership, access restriction, locked formulas, version control, independent review of changes, reconciliation and backup."]] },
  { t: "Systems availability and capacity management",
    body: [
      "Users expect systems to be there when needed and to respond quickly. Availability management plans and monitors the ability of IT services to perform when required; capacity management makes sure resources such as processing, memory, storage and network bandwidth meet current and future demand at acceptable cost.",
      "Availability is shaped by reliability (how rarely components fail), maintainability (how quickly they can be fixed) and resilience (how well the service continues when parts fail). Common measures include availability percentage, mean time between failures (MTBF) and mean time to repair (MTTR). Techniques to improve availability include redundant components, clustering, load balancing, failover, proactive maintenance and monitoring that alerts before users notice problems.",
      "Capacity management relies on monitoring current utilization, analyzing trends and forecasting demand from business plans, such as a new product launch or seasonal peaks. When forecasts show resources will run out, management plans upgrades, tuning or scaling before performance suffers. In cloud environments, auto-scaling can add capacity automatically, but it needs limits and cost monitoring. Performance tuning and workload scheduling can also make better use of existing capacity.",
      "Auditors check whether availability and capacity requirements are defined, often in service level agreements; whether monitoring covers critical systems; whether trends are reviewed and acted on; and whether incidents caused by capacity shortages are analyzed. A common finding is monitoring that exists but whose alerts nobody reviews, or growth that is noticed only when a disk fills and a service stops."
    ],
    terms: [["Availability", "The proportion of agreed time that a service is usable, often expressed as a percentage."], ["Capacity management", "Ensuring IT resources meet current and future demand cost-effectively."], ["Mean time between failures (MTBF)", "The average operating time between failures of a component or system."], ["Mean time to repair (MTTR)", "The average time needed to restore a failed component or service."]],
    example: "Storage on an order database grows 8% a month with 25% free. Capacity reports forecast it will fill in about three months, so the infrastructure team schedules an expansion for next month's maintenance window instead of waiting for an outage.",
    tip: "Capacity management is proactive: it uses trends and business forecasts to act before resources run out. Waiting for a failure is the wrong answer.",
    check: [["What data does capacity planning rely on?", "Current utilization, historical trends and forecasts of business demand."], ["Name two techniques that improve availability.", "Any two of: redundancy, clustering, load balancing, failover, proactive maintenance and monitoring."]] },
  { t: "Problem and incident management",
    body: [
      "When something goes wrong in IT operations, two related but different processes handle it. Incident management restores normal service as quickly as possible. Problem management finds and removes the underlying cause so the same incidents do not keep happening. The exam regularly tests the difference.",
      "An incident is any unplanned interruption or reduction in the quality of a service. The incident process includes detection and logging, classification and prioritization by impact and urgency, initial diagnosis, escalation to specialist teams or management when needed, resolution (often with a workaround), and closure after confirming with the user. Every incident should be recorded, even if fixed quickly, so trends can be analyzed. Service desk staff follow defined escalation rules so serious incidents reach the right people quickly.",
      "A problem is the unknown cause of one or more incidents. Problem management analyzes incident trends, performs root cause analysis using techniques such as the five whys or fishbone diagrams, documents known errors and workarounds in a known error database, and raises changes to fix the cause permanently. Problem management can be reactive, after incidents occur, or proactive, spotting weaknesses before they cause incidents.",
      "Security incidents follow a specialized response process, but the general incident process should recognize when an incident might be security-related and hand it over. The auditor checks that incidents are logged completely, prioritized consistently, resolved within agreed times, and that recurring incidents lead to problem records and permanent fixes."
    ],
    terms: [["Incident", "An unplanned interruption or reduction in the quality of an IT service."], ["Problem", "The underlying, often unknown, cause of one or more incidents."], ["Root cause analysis", "A structured investigation to identify the fundamental cause of a problem."], ["Known error", "A problem whose root cause has been identified and for which a workaround is documented."]],
    example: "A web portal crashes every Monday morning and the service desk restarts it each time. Problem management reviews the pattern, traces it to a weekend batch job that fills a log partition, and raises a change to rotate logs, ending the crashes.",
    tip: "Incident management restores service fast, even with a workaround. Problem management finds and fixes the root cause. Repeated identical incidents point to missing problem management.",
    check: [["What is the main goal of incident management?", "To restore normal service as quickly as possible and minimize business impact."], ["What should happen when the same incident recurs many times?", "A problem record should be opened and root cause analysis performed so the cause can be fixed permanently."]] },
  { t: "IT change, configuration, release and patch management",
    body: [
      "Changes are one of the most common causes of outages and control failures. Change management ensures that changes to production systems are requested, assessed, approved, tested, implemented and reviewed in a controlled way, and it is one of the IT general controls auditors test most often.",
      "A typical change process records a request, assesses impact and risk, obtains approval from appropriate owners or a change advisory board, tests the change in a non-production environment, schedules and implements it with a rollback plan, and reviews it afterward. Standard changes are low-risk, pre-approved and repeatable. Emergency changes can bypass some steps to fix urgent issues, but must still be logged and approved retrospectively. Segregation of duties is central: the person who develops a change should not be the one who approves it or moves it into production.",
      "Configuration management maintains accurate records of configuration items and their relationships, often in a configuration management database, so the impact of a change can be judged and unauthorized configuration drift can be detected. Release management bundles approved changes and deploys them in an orderly way. Patch management keeps systems updated against known vulnerabilities: it tracks vendor releases, assesses urgency, tests patches, deploys them within defined timeframes based on risk, and verifies installation.",
      "To test change management, auditors sample changes from system logs or version control and trace them to approved tickets, checking for testing evidence, approval before implementation and separation between developer and implementer. Sampling from the system, not only from the ticket list, is what reveals unauthorized changes."
    ],
    terms: [["Change advisory board (CAB)", "A group that assesses and approves or rejects significant changes."], ["Emergency change", "An urgent change implemented quickly, then documented and approved after the fact."], ["Configuration item", "Any component that must be managed to deliver an IT service, recorded with its attributes and relationships."], ["Patch management", "Identifying, testing and deploying software updates that fix vulnerabilities or defects."]],
    example: "An auditor extracts all deployments to production from the pipeline logs for a quarter and traces 25 to change tickets. Three have no ticket at all and one was approved by the same developer who wrote the code, so she reports both unauthorized changes and a segregation-of-duties weakness.",
    tip: "To find unauthorized changes, sample from what actually changed in production (system logs, version control) and trace back to approvals. Sampling only from approved tickets cannot reveal changes that never had one.",
    check: [["What must happen after an emergency change?", "It must be documented, reviewed and approved retrospectively."], ["Why test patches before deploying them?", "To make sure they do not break applications or cause outages in production."]] },
  { t: "Operational log management and IT service level management",
    body: [
      "Logs are the memory of IT operations. They record what systems did, who did it and when, supporting troubleshooting, performance analysis, security monitoring, investigations and audit. Logs only help if the right events are captured, kept long enough, protected and actually reviewed.",
      "Log management covers deciding what to log based on risk and requirements (for example logins, privilege use, configuration changes and errors), synchronizing clocks so events can be correlated, sending logs to a central store that administrators cannot alter, retaining them for the period required by policy or regulation, and reviewing them regularly or through automated alerts. Logs may contain sensitive data, so access to them must be controlled. A common weakness is local logs that overwrite themselves in days, so evidence disappears before anyone looks.",
      "IT service level management defines, agrees and monitors the level of service IT provides. A service catalog describes available services. Service level agreements (SLAs) set measurable targets with customers, such as availability, response time and incident resolution time. Operational level agreements (OLAs) are internal agreements between IT teams that support the SLA, and underpinning contracts bind external suppliers to matching commitments. Targets should be realistic, measurable and linked to business needs.",
      "Service level management includes regular reporting of actual performance against targets, reviews with customers, and improvement plans when targets are missed. The auditor checks that SLAs exist for critical services, that performance data comes from reliable monitoring rather than self-reporting, and that supplier contracts support the SLAs."
    ],
    terms: [["Service level agreement (SLA)", "An agreement between a service provider and customer that defines measurable service targets."], ["Operational level agreement (OLA)", "An internal agreement between IT teams supporting the delivery of an SLA."], ["Log retention", "How long logs are kept, set by policy, legal requirements and investigation needs."], ["Time synchronization", "Keeping system clocks aligned, for example with NTP, so events can be correlated across systems."]],
    example: "An SLA promises 99.9% monthly availability for online banking. The auditor compares the provider's monthly reports with independent monitoring data and finds two outages missing from the reports, so reported performance cannot be relied on.",
    tip: "The best evidence of SLA performance is reliable, independent measurement compared with targets. For logs, the key concerns are completeness, protection from alteration, adequate retention and actual review.",
    check: [["What is an operational level agreement?", "An internal agreement between IT teams that supports an SLA with the customer."], ["Why send logs to a central store?", "To protect them from alteration or deletion on the source system, keep them longer and allow correlation and review."]] },
  { t: "Database management: DBMS controls, integrity, normalization and DBA duties",
    body: [
      "Databases hold the organization's most valuable data, so they deserve focused audit attention. A database management system (DBMS) stores data and controls access to it, enforces integrity rules, manages concurrent use and supports backup and recovery.",
      "Relational databases organize data in tables of rows and columns linked by keys. A primary key uniquely identifies each row; a foreign key links to a primary key in another table. Referential integrity ensures a foreign key always points to a real record, for example no orders for a customer who does not exist. Entity integrity ensures each row has a unique, non-null primary key. Normalization organizes tables to store each fact once, reducing redundancy and update anomalies; denormalization intentionally adds some redundancy for performance and must be controlled so copies stay consistent. Transactions follow ACID properties (atomicity, consistency, isolation and durability) so that updates are all-or-nothing and survive failures.",
      "The database administrator (DBA) has powerful access: defining structures, managing performance, backups and recovery, and often granting access. Because DBA privileges can bypass application controls, compensating controls are essential: limiting the number of DBAs, using individual named accounts, logging DBA activity to a store they cannot modify, independent review of those logs, and change management for schema changes. Direct changes to production data outside the application should be rare, approved and logged.",
      "Other controls include access through views or roles that show users only the data they need, encryption of sensitive fields, database activity monitoring, and tested backups including transaction logs for point-in-time recovery."
    ],
    terms: [["Referential integrity", "A rule ensuring that foreign key values always match an existing primary key in the related table."], ["Normalization", "Structuring a database so each fact is stored only once, reducing redundancy and inconsistency."], ["ACID", "Properties of reliable transactions: atomicity, consistency, isolation and durability."], ["Database activity monitoring", "Tools that record and analyze database access and changes, especially by privileged users."]],
    example: "An auditor finds that three DBAs share one administrator account and that database logs are stored where the DBAs can delete them. She recommends named accounts, forwarding logs to the security team's system, and a monthly review of direct data changes by someone outside the DBA team.",
    tip: "DBAs can bypass application controls. The best answers involve logging DBA activity to a protected location and having someone independent review it.",
    check: [["What does referential integrity prevent?", "Records that refer to non-existent related records, such as orders for a customer that does not exist."], ["Why is a shared DBA account a problem?", "Actions cannot be traced to an individual, which destroys accountability."]] },
  { t: "Business impact analysis: criticality, RTO, RPO and MTD",
    body: [
      "Business resilience starts by understanding what the organization cannot do without. The business impact analysis (BIA) identifies critical business processes, the resources they depend on and the impact of losing them over time. Every later decision about continuity and recovery depends on its results.",
      "A BIA typically gathers information through interviews, questionnaires and workshops with process owners. For each process it identifies dependencies such as applications, data, people, facilities and suppliers; estimates financial, operational, legal, regulatory and reputational impact over time; and ranks processes by criticality. Senior management should review and approve the results, since they reflect business priorities rather than IT preferences.",
      "The BIA sets key recovery measures. The maximum tolerable downtime (MTD), also called maximum tolerable period of disruption, is the longest a process can be unavailable before the damage becomes unacceptable. The recovery time objective (RTO) is the target time to restore a system or process after disruption, and must be shorter than the MTD. The recovery point objective (RPO) is the maximum acceptable data loss, measured in time back from the incident, and it drives how often data must be backed up or replicated. The service delivery objective describes the reduced service level acceptable during recovery.",
      "Low RTOs and RPOs are expensive, requiring hot sites, replication and redundancy, while higher values allow cheaper options. The BIA helps balance cost against impact. It should be updated when the business or systems change significantly."
    ],
    terms: [["Business impact analysis (BIA)", "An analysis of critical processes, their dependencies and the impact of disruption over time."], ["Recovery time objective (RTO)", "The target time within which a system or process must be restored after a disruption."], ["Recovery point objective (RPO)", "The maximum acceptable amount of data loss, measured in time before the disruption."], ["Maximum tolerable downtime (MTD)", "The longest time a process can be disrupted before the impact becomes unacceptable."]],
    example: "An online retailer's BIA finds that order processing loses significant revenue after four hours and cannot lose more than 15 minutes of orders. It sets an RTO of two hours and an RPO of 15 minutes, which leads to database replication to a second site rather than nightly backups alone.",
    tip: "RPO is about data loss and drives backup frequency; RTO is about time to restore and drives recovery site choice. The BIA comes first, before choosing strategies or sites.",
    check: [["Which measure determines how often data must be backed up?", "The recovery point objective (RPO)."], ["What is the relationship between RTO and MTD?", "The RTO must be shorter than the MTD so recovery happens before the damage becomes unacceptable."]] },
  { t: "System resiliency and data backup, storage and restoration",
    body: [
      "Resilience means keeping services running, or restoring them quickly, when components fail or disasters strike. It combines designs that avoid single points of failure with backups that allow data to be recovered.",
      "Resilient designs use redundancy at several levels: RAID for disks, redundant power supplies and network paths, clustered servers, load balancers that spread traffic and remove failed nodes, and replication of data to another site or cloud region. RAID 1 mirrors disks; RAID 5 and 6 use parity to survive one or two disk failures; RAID 10 combines mirroring and striping. RAID 0 only stripes and has no redundancy. Uninterruptible power supplies and generators protect against power loss. Fault tolerance keeps a service running through a failure, while high availability minimizes downtime through quick failover.",
      "Backups protect against data loss from failure, error, corruption and ransomware. A full backup copies everything; an incremental backup copies changes since the last backup of any kind, making backups fast but restores slower; a differential backup copies changes since the last full backup, so restores need only the full backup and the latest differential. Backup frequency follows the RPO. Copies should be stored offsite or in another region, and at least one copy should be offline or immutable so ransomware cannot encrypt it. Backups need encryption, access control and documented retention.",
      "Restoration is what matters. Organizations should regularly test restores of files, systems and full applications to confirm backups are complete and usable within the RTO. The auditor reviews backup schedules against RPOs, job logs, offsite and immutable storage, and evidence of successful restore tests."
    ],
    terms: [["Incremental backup", "A backup of data changed since the last backup of any type."], ["Differential backup", "A backup of data changed since the last full backup."], ["Immutable backup", "A backup copy that cannot be changed or deleted for a set period, protecting against ransomware."], ["Single point of failure", "A component whose failure stops the whole service because it has no redundancy."]],
    example: "A law firm backs up nightly to a disk in the same server room. After ransomware encrypts both servers and the backup disk, recovery takes weeks. The new design keeps daily backups in a cloud region with immutability and tests a restore each month.",
    tip: "A successful backup job does not prove recoverability. Periodic restore tests are the best evidence that backups work, and at least one copy should be offsite and protected from ransomware.",
    check: [["Which backup type needs only the last full backup and one other set to restore?", "Differential."], ["Why is RAID not a substitute for backups?", "RAID protects against disk failure but copies deletions, corruption and ransomware encryption instantly, so it cannot restore earlier data."]] },
  { t: "Business continuity and disaster recovery plans: recovery sites, testing and maintenance",
    body: [
      "A business continuity plan (BCP) describes how the organization keeps critical business processes going during and after a disruption. A disaster recovery plan (DRP) is the IT part: how systems, data and infrastructure are restored. Both depend on the BIA and on senior management support.",
      "Plans should define scope and assumptions, roles and responsibilities, activation criteria and who declares a disaster, communication plans for staff, customers, regulators and media, contact lists, recovery procedures in priority order, and arrangements with suppliers. Copies must be available when primary systems and buildings are not. Teams usually include incident management, damage assessment, recovery, and business resumption roles.",
      "Recovery site options balance cost and speed. A hot site is fully equipped with current data and can take over within hours. A warm site has some equipment and connectivity but needs data restored and configuration, taking days. A cold site has only space, power and cooling, taking weeks. Mirrored sites run in parallel with near-zero downtime at high cost. Cloud-based disaster recovery can provide hot or warm capacity on demand. Reciprocal agreements with other organizations are cheap but hard to enforce and test.",
      "Testing proves the plan works and trains people. In increasing order of realism and disruption, tests include checklist reviews, tabletop or walk-through exercises, simulations, parallel tests (bringing up recovery systems without stopping production) and full interruption tests (actually shutting down primary operations). Results are documented, gaps fixed and plans retested. Plans must be maintained after organizational, system and staff changes and reviewed at least annually. An untested or outdated plan is one of the most common audit findings."
    ],
    terms: [["Hot site", "A fully equipped recovery site with current data that can resume operations within hours."], ["Cold site", "A recovery facility with space and utilities but no equipment or data."], ["Tabletop exercise", "A discussion-based test where participants walk through a scenario to check their understanding of the plan."], ["Full interruption test", "A test that shuts down primary operations and runs entirely at the recovery site; the most realistic and most disruptive."]],
    example: "An insurer's DR test restores its policy system at a warm site but takes 30 hours against a 12-hour RTO because network changes were missing from the plan. The team updates the runbook, adds pre-staged network configuration and passes a retest within 10 hours.",
    tip: "The BIA comes first, then strategy, plan, testing and maintenance. After a failed test, update the plan and retest; after major changes, update the plan.",
    check: [["Which test is the most disruptive?", "A full interruption test, because it actually stops primary operations."], ["Why are reciprocal agreements weak recovery options?", "The partner may lack capacity when needed, they are hard to enforce and they are rarely tested."]] },
  { t: "Information asset security policies, frameworks, standards and guidelines",
    body: [
      "Protecting information assets starts with clear direction. An information security program sets out what must be protected, how much protection it needs and who is responsible, and it gives auditors criteria to test against.",
      "The core document is the information security policy, approved by senior management, which states objectives, scope, roles and the commitment to protect information. Supporting policies cover topics such as acceptable use, access control, classification, encryption, remote work and incident response. Standards set mandatory specifics, such as minimum password or key lengths. Security baselines or hardening standards define minimum secure configurations for each platform, often based on recognized benchmarks. Procedures describe how to perform tasks, and guidelines offer recommended practice. Everything should be reviewed regularly and approved exceptions tracked with owners and expiry dates.",
      "Frameworks give structure and a common language. ISO/IEC 27001 specifies requirements for an information security management system (ISMS) and can be certified; ISO/IEC 27002 gives guidance on controls. The NIST Cybersecurity Framework organizes outcomes into functions including govern, identify, protect, detect, respond and recover. The CIS Critical Security Controls give a prioritized set of technical safeguards. COBIT connects security to overall IT governance. Organizations often map their controls to several frameworks at once.",
      "Roles matter: senior management is accountable, the information security manager or CISO runs the program, data owners decide protection needs, custodians implement controls and all users follow policy. The auditor checks that policies exist and are approved, current, communicated and enforced, and that baselines are applied consistently, for example by comparing actual server settings with the hardening standard."
    ],
    terms: [["Information security management system (ISMS)", "A systematic approach to managing information security risks through policies, processes and controls, as defined in ISO/IEC 27001."], ["Security baseline", "The minimum set of security settings required for a type of system."], ["Policy exception", "A formally approved and time-limited deviation from a policy or standard, with an accountable owner."], ["Hardening", "Reducing a system's attack surface by removing unneeded services and applying secure settings."]],
    example: "An auditor compares 30 servers with the company's hardening standard using a configuration scanning tool. Six have remote administration services enabled that the standard prohibits and no approved exceptions exist, so she reports the deviation and asks for either remediation or formal exceptions.",
    tip: "A baseline makes configuration consistent and testable, but it does not eliminate the need for vulnerability scanning or monitoring. Exceptions must be formal, approved and time-limited.",
    check: [["Which framework defines certifiable requirements for an ISMS?", "ISO/IEC 27001."], ["What should an auditor check about policies?", "That they are approved by management, current, communicated to staff, enforced and matched by actual practice."]] },
  { t: "Physical and environmental controls",
    body: [
      "Logical controls are useless if someone can walk out with a server or if a flood destroys the data center. Physical and environmental controls protect facilities, equipment and the people who work there.",
      "Physical access controls work in layers: site perimeter such as fences and lighting, building entrances with guards or receptionists, and internal secure areas such as data centers and wiring closets. Access methods include badges, PINs, biometrics and locks, and access should be granted by need and reviewed regularly. Mantraps, or access control vestibules, allow one person through at a time and stop tailgating. Visitors should be registered, badged and escorted. CCTV and alarms detect and record events, and access logs support investigations. Data centers should have minimal signage that reveals their purpose.",
      "Environmental controls protect equipment from power, temperature, humidity, water and fire. Power protection includes uninterruptible power supplies for short outages and conditioning, plus generators for longer ones, and both need regular testing. Heating, ventilation and air conditioning keep temperature and humidity within range. Water detection sensors under raised floors warn of leaks. Fire protection combines detection (smoke and heat detectors) with suppression. Wet-pipe sprinklers hold water in the pipes; dry-pipe and pre-action systems keep pipes dry until triggered, reducing accidental discharge, and pre-action is common in staffed data centers. Gas-based clean agent systems suppress fire without water but need safety procedures; carbon dioxide is dangerous in occupied rooms.",
      "Equipment should be located away from hazards, and media and equipment leaving the site should be authorized and protected. The auditor inspects facilities, reviews access lists and logs, and checks maintenance and test records for UPS, generators and suppression systems."
    ],
    terms: [["Mantrap", "An entry area with two doors that lets only one authenticated person pass at a time, preventing tailgating."], ["Tailgating", "Following an authorized person through a secured door without authenticating."], ["Pre-action sprinkler", "A sprinkler system whose pipes stay dry until a detector triggers, reducing accidental water damage."], ["Uninterruptible power supply (UPS)", "A battery-based device that provides short-term power and conditioning during outages."]],
    example: "During a data center walkthrough, the auditor sees staff hold the door for colleagues and finds that 12 people on the access list left the company last year. She recommends a mantrap, a quarterly access list review by the facilities owner and awareness training on tailgating.",
    tip: "A camera or sign-in sheet detects or deters; a mantrap prevents tailgating. For staffed data centers, pre-action sprinklers balance safety and water damage risk.",
    check: [["Why test generators and UPS units regularly?", "To confirm they will actually work and carry the load during a real power failure."], ["Why is carbon dioxide suppression unsuitable for occupied rooms?", "It displaces oxygen and can harm or kill people in the room."]] },
  { t: "Identity and access management: authentication, authorization, provisioning and access reviews",
    body: [
      "Identity and access management (IAM) ensures that the right people and systems have the right access to the right resources, and no more. Access control failures are among the most common audit findings, so this topic is heavily tested.",
      "Identification is claiming an identity, such as a user ID. Authentication proves it using factors: something you know (password), something you have (token, phone, security key) or something you are (biometric). Multifactor authentication combines different types; two passwords are still one factor type. Authorization decides what an authenticated identity may do, based on principles such as least privilege and need to know. Models include role-based access control, where permissions attach to job roles, and attribute-based access control, where rules consider attributes such as department or location. Accountability comes from unique IDs and logging, which is why shared accounts are a finding.",
      "Provisioning follows the joiner, mover, leaver cycle. Joiners receive access based on an approved request, ideally through predefined roles. Movers should lose old access when they gain new, avoiding privilege creep. Leavers must be disabled promptly, best done by linking HR termination events to automated deprovisioning. Privileged accounts, such as administrators, need extra controls: separate admin accounts, multifactor authentication, just-in-time elevation, session recording and regular review. Single sign-on and federation using protocols such as SAML or OpenID Connect reduce password sprawl but make the identity provider critical.",
      "Periodic access reviews, or recertifications, have data or application owners confirm that each user's access is still appropriate. The auditor tests by comparing HR leaver lists to active accounts, sampling new and changed access for approval, reviewing privileged accounts and checking that review results are acted on."
    ],
    terms: [["Least privilege", "Granting users only the minimum access needed to perform their duties."], ["Multifactor authentication (MFA)", "Authentication that combines two or more different factor types."], ["Privilege creep", "Gradual accumulation of access rights as users change roles without removing old access."], ["Access recertification", "A periodic review in which owners confirm that users' access remains appropriate."]],
    example: "Comparing the HR leaver list with directory accounts, an auditor finds 14 former staff still enabled, two of whom logged in after leaving. The root cause is a manual email-based leaver process, so she recommends automated deprovisioning triggered by HR and monthly reconciliation.",
    tip: "Owners review access; administrators implement. For leavers, the best control links HR terminations to automatic account removal, supported by periodic reviews.",
    check: [["Is a password plus a PIN multifactor authentication?", "No, both are something you know, so it is a single factor type."], ["What causes privilege creep and how is it controlled?", "Users keep old access when changing roles; it is controlled by removing access on transfer and by periodic access reviews."]] },
  { t: "Network and endpoint security: firewalls, segmentation, IDS/IPS, remote access and EDR",
    body: [
      "Networks connect everything, which also lets attackers move from one system to another. Network and endpoint security aim to limit who can reach what, detect malicious activity and protect the devices people use.",
      "Firewalls filter traffic based on rules. Packet filters look at addresses and ports; stateful firewalls track connections; next-generation and application firewalls understand applications and can inspect content. Web application firewalls protect web apps from attacks such as injection. Rule sets should deny by default, allow only what is needed, have a documented business owner for each rule, log important traffic and be reviewed periodically to remove unused, overly broad or shadowed rules. Segmentation divides the network into zones, such as a DMZ for internet-facing servers, separate segments for payment systems or IoT devices, and restricted admin networks, so that a compromise in one zone cannot easily spread. Zero trust takes this further by verifying every request rather than trusting network location.",
      "Intrusion detection systems (IDS) monitor traffic or hosts and alert on suspicious activity; intrusion prevention systems (IPS) sit inline and can block it. Both need tuning to reduce false positives and someone to act on alerts. Remote access should use encrypted connections such as VPNs or zero trust network access, with multifactor authentication and posture checks on devices.",
      "Endpoints include laptops, desktops, servers and mobile devices. Controls include hardened configurations, patching, host firewalls, anti-malware and endpoint detection and response (EDR) tools that record behavior and help investigate and contain threats, disk encryption and application allow listing. The auditor reviews firewall rule sets and change records, segmentation diagrams, IDS/IPS alert handling, remote access configuration and endpoint coverage reports."
    ],
    terms: [["Network segmentation", "Dividing a network into zones with controlled traffic between them to limit the spread of attacks."], ["Intrusion prevention system (IPS)", "An inline device that detects and blocks malicious network traffic."], ["Endpoint detection and response (EDR)", "Software that monitors endpoint behavior to detect, investigate and contain threats."], ["Demilitarized zone (DMZ)", "A network segment that hosts internet-facing services, separated from the internal network."]],
    example: "Reviewing a firewall, an auditor finds an allow-any rule added during an outage two years ago and never removed, plus 60 rules with no owner. She recommends removing the broad rule, assigning owners and scheduling semiannual rule reviews.",
    tip: "IDS detects and alerts; IPS sits inline and blocks. Firewall rule sets should deny by default, and rules near the top that allow any traffic defeat everything below them.",
    check: [["What is the main difference between an IDS and an IPS?", "An IDS only detects and alerts; an IPS is inline and can block traffic."], ["Why segment a network?", "To limit how far an attacker or malware can spread after compromising one system and to protect sensitive zones."]] },
  { t: "Data loss prevention and data encryption",
    body: [
      "Data can leave an organization by mistake or on purpose: an email to the wrong person, a file uploaded to personal cloud storage, a lost laptop, or a deliberate theft. Data loss prevention (DLP) and encryption are two main technical defenses.",
      "DLP tools identify sensitive data using patterns (such as card numbers), keywords, fingerprints of specific documents or classification labels, then monitor or block it. Network DLP inspects traffic such as email and web uploads, endpoint DLP controls actions on devices such as copying to USB drives, and cloud DLP scans cloud services. DLP depends on data classification and needs tuning to avoid false positives. Many deployments start in monitor-only mode, which is sensible for tuning, but a DLP tool whose alerts nobody reviews gives little real protection.",
      "Encryption turns readable data into ciphertext that only key holders can read. Symmetric encryption, such as AES, uses one shared key and is fast, so it is used for bulk data. Asymmetric encryption, such as RSA or elliptic curve, uses a public and private key pair and is used for key exchange and digital signatures. Data at rest is protected with full-disk, database or file encryption; data in transit with protocols such as TLS and VPNs. Hashing is a one-way function that proves integrity but is not encryption.",
      "Encryption is only as strong as key management: generating keys securely, storing them separately from the data (for example in a hardware security module or key management service), controlling access, rotating keys, and securely destroying them at end of life. The auditor checks that sensitive data is encrypted per policy, algorithms and key lengths are current, and keys are managed and protected."
    ],
    terms: [["Data loss prevention (DLP)", "Tools and processes that detect and prevent unauthorized transmission or storage of sensitive data."], ["Symmetric encryption", "Encryption that uses the same secret key to encrypt and decrypt."], ["Key management", "Controls over the generation, storage, distribution, rotation and destruction of cryptographic keys."], ["Hardware security module (HSM)", "A tamper-resistant device that securely generates, stores and uses cryptographic keys."]],
    example: "An auditor finds a customer database encrypted, but the encryption key is stored in a configuration file on the same server, readable by application administrators. She reports that encryption provides little protection and recommends moving keys to a key management service with restricted access.",
    tip: "Encryption is only as good as its key management. A key stored next to the data it protects is a common finding.",
    check: [["Why is DLP dependent on data classification?", "The tool needs to know which data is sensitive in order to recognize and protect it."], ["Which type of encryption is typically used for bulk data, and why?", "Symmetric encryption, because it is much faster than asymmetric encryption."]] },
  { t: "Public key infrastructure (PKI) and digital signatures",
    body: [
      "Asymmetric cryptography needs a way to trust that a public key really belongs to the person or system it claims. Public key infrastructure (PKI) provides that trust through digital certificates issued by trusted certificate authorities.",
      "A digital certificate binds a public key to an identity, such as a website name or a person, and is signed by a certificate authority (CA). It contains the subject, the public key, the issuer, validity dates, a serial number and allowed key uses. A registration authority (RA) verifies identities before the CA issues certificates. Root CAs sit at the top of the trust chain, usually kept offline, and issuing CAs below them sign day-to-day certificates. Certificates can be revoked before they expire, for example when a private key is compromised, and relying parties check status through certificate revocation lists (CRLs) or the online certificate status protocol (OCSP). A certificate practice statement describes how the CA operates.",
      "Digital signatures provide integrity, authentication and non-repudiation. The signer hashes the message and encrypts the hash with their private key. The recipient decrypts the signature with the signer's public key and compares it with a fresh hash of the message. A match proves the message was not changed and that only the private key holder could have signed it. Confidentiality is a separate service: to keep a message secret, encrypt it with the recipient's public key (in practice, a symmetric key protected that way).",
      "Private keys must be protected, ideally in hardware tokens or HSMs. The auditor reviews CA controls, certificate inventories and expiry monitoring, revocation processes and private key protection. Expired certificates causing outages are a common operational finding."
    ],
    terms: [["Certificate authority (CA)", "A trusted entity that issues and signs digital certificates."], ["Digital signature", "A hash of a message encrypted with the signer's private key, providing integrity, authentication and non-repudiation."], ["Certificate revocation list (CRL)", "A list published by a CA of certificates revoked before their expiry."], ["Non-repudiation", "Assurance that a party cannot credibly deny having sent or signed a message."]],
    example: "A company signs electronic purchase orders with employees' private keys stored on smart cards. When a supplier disputes an order, the company verifies the signature with the employee's certificate, proving the order came from that card and was not altered.",
    tip: "Sign with your private key; verify with the sender's public key. Encrypt for confidentiality with the recipient's public key. Symmetric keys cannot give non-repudiation because both sides hold them.",
    check: [["Which key verifies a digital signature?", "The signer's public key."], ["When should a certificate be revoked?", "When its private key is compromised, the holder's details change or it is no longer needed before expiry."]] },
  { t: "Cloud, virtualized, mobile, wireless and IoT environments",
    body: [
      "Modern IT runs on shared and distributed platforms: virtual machines, containers, cloud services, phones, wireless networks and connected devices. Each brings specific risks the auditor must understand.",
      "Virtualization runs many virtual machines on one physical host through a hypervisor. It improves efficiency but concentrates risk: a hypervisor compromise affects every guest, and virtual machines can be created quickly without proper controls. Controls include hardening and patching hypervisors, restricting management consoles, separating workloads of different sensitivity and managing images and templates. Containers package applications with their dependencies and need secure images, registries and orchestration.",
      "Cloud services follow a shared responsibility model. In infrastructure as a service (IaaS), the provider secures the physical infrastructure and hypervisor while the customer secures operating systems, applications, identities and data. In platform as a service (PaaS) the provider also manages the platform, and in software as a service (SaaS) the provider runs the application while the customer manages users, access, configuration and data. In every model the customer stays accountable for its data. Common cloud findings are misconfigured storage, excessive permissions and missing logs. Assurance comes from contracts, SOC reports and certifications, and the customer's own configuration monitoring.",
      "Mobile devices are easily lost and mix personal and business use; mobile device management (MDM) enforces screen locks, encryption, app controls and remote wipe, often through a separate work container. Wireless networks should use current protections such as WPA3 or WPA2-Enterprise with strong authentication, and guest networks should be separated. Internet of Things (IoT) devices often have weak default credentials, limited patching and long lifespans, so they should be inventoried, placed in isolated network segments and monitored."
    ],
    terms: [["Shared responsibility model", "The division of security duties between a cloud provider and its customer, which varies by service model."], ["Hypervisor", "Software that creates and runs virtual machines on a physical host."], ["Mobile device management (MDM)", "Tools that enforce security policies on mobile devices, including encryption and remote wipe."], ["Internet of Things (IoT)", "Network-connected physical devices such as sensors, cameras and controllers."]],
    example: "An auditor reviewing a cloud account finds a storage bucket holding customer exports configured for public access. The provider's controls are fine; the misconfiguration is the customer's responsibility, so she recommends configuration monitoring that alerts on public storage.",
    tip: "In the cloud, the customer always keeps accountability for its data, identities and configuration. Most cloud breaches come from customer misconfiguration, not provider failure.",
    check: [["In SaaS, who manages user access to the application?", "The customer."], ["Why isolate IoT devices on their own network segment?", "They are often poorly secured and hard to patch, so isolation limits what an attacker can reach through them."]] },
  { t: "Security awareness training and information system attack methods",
    body: [
      "People are both a target and a defense. Many attacks start by tricking someone, and trained staff who spot and report attempts stop many of them. Understanding how attacks work helps the auditor judge whether controls address real threats.",
      "Social engineering manipulates people. Phishing sends deceptive emails to steal credentials or deliver malware; spear phishing targets specific people; whaling targets executives; vishing and smishing use voice calls and text messages. Business email compromise uses spoofed or hijacked executive or supplier email to request payments or data. Pretexting invents a story to obtain information, and tailgating exploits courtesy to enter secure areas.",
      "Technical attacks include malware such as ransomware, which encrypts data and demands payment; trojans and worms; password attacks such as credential stuffing and brute force; denial-of-service attacks that overwhelm systems; on-path (man-in-the-middle) attacks that intercept communications; and web application attacks such as SQL injection and cross-site scripting that exploit poor input handling. Advanced persistent threats are well-resourced attackers who stay hidden in a network for long periods. Insider threats come from employees or contractors, whether malicious or careless. Supply chain attacks compromise a trusted vendor or software update to reach its customers.",
      "A security awareness program gives all staff regular, role-appropriate training, with extra training for high-risk roles such as finance and administrators. It uses phishing simulations, clear ways to report suspicious messages and messages from leadership. Effectiveness is measured by behavior, such as simulation click and report rates over time and reported incidents, not just training completion. Process controls, such as call-back verification for payment changes, back up training."
    ],
    terms: [["Phishing", "Deceptive messages that try to trick recipients into revealing information or running malicious content."], ["Business email compromise (BEC)", "Fraud using spoofed or compromised business email to trick staff into sending money or data."], ["Ransomware", "Malware that encrypts or steals data and demands payment for its release."], ["SQL injection", "An attack that inserts malicious database commands through poorly validated input."]],
    example: "An accounts payable clerk receives an email from a supplier's real address, compromised by an attacker, asking to change bank details. Following training and procedure, she calls the supplier on the number already on file, discovers the fraud and reports it to security.",
    tip: "Measure awareness by behavior change, such as phishing simulation click and report trends, rather than attendance. For payment fraud, independent call-back verification is a strong process control.",
    check: [["What distinguishes spear phishing from ordinary phishing?", "It targets specific individuals or groups with tailored, convincing content."], ["Which control best counters business email compromise requests to change bank details?", "Verifying the request through an independent, known channel such as a call-back to a number already on file, plus dual approval."]] },
  { t: "Security testing tools and techniques: vulnerability scanning, penetration testing and configuration review",
    body: [
      "Security testing finds weaknesses before attackers do and gives evidence about whether controls work. The auditor may perform some tests, rely on tests done by others, or review the organization's own testing program.",
      "Vulnerability scanning uses automated tools to identify known weaknesses such as missing patches, weak configurations and exposed services. Authenticated (credentialed) scans log in to systems and give more complete, accurate results than unauthenticated scans. Scans should run regularly and after significant changes, and findings should be prioritized by severity and exposure, assigned to owners, fixed within defined timeframes and rescanned to confirm. Scanners produce false positives, so results need validation.",
      "Penetration testing goes further: skilled testers try to exploit weaknesses to show what an attacker could actually achieve. Tests can be external or internal, and testers may be given no knowledge (black box), partial knowledge (gray box) or full knowledge (white box) of the environment. Before any test there must be written authorization from management, an agreed scope and rules of engagement covering timing, targets, exclusions, contacts and data handling. Red team exercises simulate a real adversary to test detection and response as well as prevention. Other techniques include social engineering tests, wireless testing and application security testing, such as static and dynamic analysis of code.",
      "Configuration review compares system settings with hardening baselines, often with automated compliance tools. Password cracking tests, with approval, can reveal weak passwords. The auditor evaluates whether testing is risk-based, independent where needed, and followed by timely remediation, and whether testers are qualified."
    ],
    terms: [["Vulnerability scan", "An automated check that identifies known weaknesses on systems and applications."], ["Penetration test", "An authorized, controlled attempt to exploit vulnerabilities to demonstrate real impact."], ["Rules of engagement", "The agreed scope, timing, methods, contacts and limits for a security test."], ["Authenticated scan", "A vulnerability scan that logs in to systems for more complete and accurate results."]],
    example: "A quarterly scan finds 40 critical vulnerabilities on internet-facing servers. The auditor checks the remediation tracker and finds 15 still open after 90 days against a 30-day policy, with no approved exceptions, and reports the remediation gap.",
    tip: "A vulnerability scan identifies; a penetration test exploits to prove impact. No penetration test should start without written authorization and agreed rules of engagement.",
    check: [["Why are authenticated scans preferred?", "They can inspect installed software and settings, giving more complete and accurate results with fewer false positives."], ["What is the difference between black-box and white-box testing?", "Black-box testers start with no internal knowledge; white-box testers have full information about the environment or code."]] },
  { t: "Security monitoring: logs, SIEM and alert management",
    body: [
      "Preventive controls eventually fail, so organizations must be able to detect attacks and misuse quickly. Security monitoring collects and analyzes events from across the environment and turns them into alerts that people investigate.",
      "Sources include firewalls, IDS/IPS, endpoint detection and response tools, servers, directories, databases, applications, cloud platforms and physical access systems. A security information and event management (SIEM) system collects logs centrally, normalizes them into a common format, correlates events across sources and applies rules or analytics to raise alerts, such as many failed logins followed by a success from an unusual country. User and entity behavior analytics (UEBA) look for deviations from normal behavior. Security orchestration, automation and response (SOAR) tools automate routine steps such as enriching alerts or isolating a device.",
      "Monitoring only works if it is designed and operated well. Important events must actually be logged and forwarded, clocks synchronized, and logs protected from tampering and kept according to retention requirements. Use cases, meaning the specific threats to detect, should be based on risk. Rules need tuning: too many false positives cause alert fatigue and real attacks get missed. A security operations center (SOC), internal or outsourced, triages alerts according to documented procedures and escalates confirmed incidents to incident response. Metrics such as mean time to detect and respond show performance.",
      "The auditor checks log source coverage against the asset inventory, reviews use cases and tuning, samples alerts to see whether they were investigated and closed appropriately, and checks that privileged activity, including that of security staff, is monitored."
    ],
    terms: [["SIEM", "Security information and event management: a system that collects, correlates and analyzes security events and raises alerts."], ["Correlation rule", "Logic that links related events from different sources to identify suspicious patterns."], ["Alert fatigue", "Desensitization caused by too many alerts, especially false positives, leading to missed real threats."], ["Security operations center (SOC)", "A team that monitors, triages and responds to security alerts."]],
    example: "An auditor compares the SIEM's log sources with the asset inventory and finds that the payroll database and two domain controllers send no logs. She also samples 20 high-severity alerts and finds five closed without investigation notes.",
    tip: "A SIEM with missing log sources or unreviewed alerts gives false comfort. Coverage, tuning and evidence that alerts are investigated are what make monitoring effective.",
    check: [["What does correlation add over reviewing single logs?", "It links events across sources to reveal patterns, such as an attack moving between systems, that no single log shows."], ["How can an auditor test whether monitoring is working?", "By checking log source coverage, reviewing use cases and sampling alerts for timely, documented investigation."]] },
  { t: "Security incident response management, evidence collection and forensics",
    body: [
      "Even strong defenses will sometimes fail. Incident response management limits damage, restores operations and learns from each event. An organization that prepares in advance responds faster and more consistently than one that improvises.",
      "Incident response follows a lifecycle. Preparation includes a policy and plan, a response team with defined roles, contact lists (legal, communications, management, law enforcement, regulators), tools and playbooks for common scenarios, and exercises. Detection and analysis confirm that an incident has occurred and assess its scope and severity. Containment stops the spread, for example by isolating hosts or disabling accounts. Eradication removes the cause, such as malware and attacker access. Recovery restores systems, for example from clean backups, and monitors for reinfection. The post-incident review captures lessons learned and improvements. Legal and regulatory requirements, including breach notification deadlines, must be built into the plan.",
      "Evidence collection supports investigation and possible legal action. Evidence should be collected in order of volatility, starting with the most volatile, such as memory and network connections, before disk contents and archived data. Forensic copies should be bit-for-bit images, verified with hash values, and analysis should be done on copies, never the original. Chain of custody documentation records who collected, handled, transferred and stored each item, and when, so it can be shown that evidence was not altered. Forensic work requires trained staff or specialists; an untrained person can easily destroy evidence.",
      "The auditor reviews whether the plan exists, is tested and current, whether incidents are logged and classified, whether lessons learned lead to changes, and whether evidence handling procedures are defined."
    ],
    terms: [["Containment", "Actions that limit the spread and impact of an incident."], ["Order of volatility", "The sequence for collecting evidence, from most short-lived (memory) to most persistent (archives)."], ["Chain of custody", "Documentation showing who handled evidence, when and how, to prove it was not altered."], ["Forensic image", "An exact bit-for-bit copy of storage media, verified with a hash, used for analysis."]],
    example: "After ransomware hits a file server, the team isolates it from the network, captures memory, images the disk and records hashes and chain of custody. They confirm backups are clean and the attacker's accounts are removed before restoring, then hold a lessons-learned review that leads to MFA on remote access.",
    tip: "Collect evidence in order of volatility, work on verified copies and keep chain of custody. Before restoring after ransomware, make sure the attacker's access is removed and backups are clean.",
    check: [["Why analyze a copy rather than the original evidence?", "To preserve the original unaltered, so its integrity can be proven with hash values and chain of custody."], ["What is the purpose of the post-incident review?", "To identify what worked, what did not and what to change to prevent or better handle future incidents."]] },
], { reviewed: "2026-09-29" });
